const e=[{id:1,code:"OPS-MIS-101",icon:"ShieldCheck",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Mission, principles and legal basis of civil defense work",subtitle:"Why civil defense exists and the principles it works by",summary:"Civil defense is a humanitarian service that protects people from fire, natural disasters and emergencies. In this lesson you learn its international tasks, the principles of its work and what is set by law and by the instructions of the competent authority.",blocks:[{heading:"What is civil defense?",body:"Civil defense is all the work and preparation that protects civilians from danger, helps them recover from the immediate effects of a disaster and provides the conditions they need to survive. It is not only firefighting: it also covers warning, rescue, first aid, evacuation and shelter. Civil Defense staff stay ready day and night because emergencies give no notice. The public is an important part of this work: anyone who calls 115 correctly, keeps a road clear or alerts an elderly neighbor helps protect lives. This lesson opens the section on the work of Civil Defense, and the following lessons build on it."},{heading:"The international tasks (Protocol I, Article 61)",body:`Additional Protocol I to the Geneva Conventions (1977), Article 61, defines civil defense as the performance of humanitarian tasks to protect the civilian population. These are the tasks:

• Warning.
• Evacuation.
• Management of shelters.
• Management of blackout measures.
• Rescue.
• Medical services, including first aid.
• Firefighting.
• Detection and marking of danger areas.
• Decontamination and similar protective measures.
• Emergency accommodation and supplies.
• Emergency help to restore order in distressed areas.
• Emergency repair of indispensable public utilities.
• Emergency disposal of the dead.
• Help in preserving objects essential for survival.
• Complementary activities, including planning and organization.`},{heading:"Principles of the work",body:`Civil defense work rests on several principles:

• Human life first: lives, then property, then the environment.
• Team safety: an injured rescuer cannot rescue anyone else.
• Impartiality and equality: help is given to everyone according to need, without discrimination.
• Organization and command: every incident has one commander and a clear plan.
• Coordination: with the ambulance service (122), the police (104) and other bodies.
• Prevention before response: inspection, awareness and training protect more lives than firefighting alone.
• Honesty and protection of the personal information of affected people.`},{heading:"Protection and the international sign",body:"Protocol I requires that civilian civil defense organizations, their staff, buildings and equipment be respected and protected so that they can carry out their humanitarian tasks. The international distinctive sign of civil defense is a blue equilateral triangle on an orange background (Article 66). Its purpose is to make civil defense staff and vehicles easy to recognize. Using it for other purposes is not allowed and damages public trust in the sign. For an ordinary citizen this means: a Civil Defense member serves everyone, and obstructing their work or pretending to be one of them can put lives at risk."},{heading:"Local law and instructions",body:"In every country, one or more laws set out the tasks and powers of civil defense and the duties of citizens and institutions, such as safety requirements in buildings or the duty to help during a disaster. This lesson does not give the names or numbers of local laws, because the Directorate has not yet confirmed them officially. Whenever you need legal information, follow the instructions of Civil Defense and the competent authority and ask for the official published text, not what circulates on social media. The rule is: what Civil Defense does, it does on the basis of the law and by order of the competent authority."}],steps:[{title:"Recognize the hazard",detail:"Work out which civil defense task this incident involves: fire, rescue, evacuation or relief."},{title:"Protect life",detail:"Your safety and other people's come first; property comes later. Do not put yourself in danger.",critical:!0},{title:"Report",detail:"Call 115 (and 122 if people are hurt): the location, what happened, how many people and the dangers.",critical:!0},{title:"Follow instructions",detail:"When the team arrives, follow the instructions of the incident commander and the competent authority."}],warnings:["Do not use the civil defense sign or uniform for personal purposes: it weakens public trust in the sign.","Do not block Civil Defense vehicles or crowd around the crews: every minute of delay can cost a life."],tips:["Remember 115 for fire and rescue, 122 for the ambulance and 104 for the police.","Take legal information only from the official text and Civil Defense instructions.","If you want to volunteer, ask the Civil Defense Directorate whether training courses are available."],activity:{prompt:'A friend says: "Civil defense only puts out fires." What do you answer?',options:[{text:"True, its only task is firefighting.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: Article 61 lists fifteen tasks, including warning, rescue and shelter."},{text:"It also covers rescue, warning, evacuation, first aid and shelter, not just fire.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: this matches the international list of tasks."},{text:"Its task is only to protect government buildings.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: its task is to protect the whole civilian population."},{text:"It only works in wartime.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: Civil Defense responds every day to fires, accidents and natural disasters."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"In a village the river rose and houses were flooded. A Civil Defense team arrived. Based on the Article 61 tasks, which task comes first?",options:[{text:"Warning people and rescuing those in danger.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: life comes first, so warning and rescue lead."},{text:"Repairing the village roads.",correct:!1,feedback:"That is a later task, once no one is left in danger."},{text:"Counting the damage to property.",correct:!1,feedback:"Property comes after life."}]},{prompt:"People have left their homes but have nowhere to stay. What is the next task?",options:[{text:"Emergency accommodation and supplies, coordinated with relief bodies.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: this is one of the Article 61 tasks and the relief and shelter department coordinates it."},{text:"Order people back to their homes.",correct:!1,feedback:"Return only after the competent authority declares the area safe."},{text:"The team leaves and the work is over.",correct:!1,feedback:"The work does not end with rescue: shelter and supplies are tasks too."}]}],quiz:[{question:"What is the international distinctive sign of civil defense?",options:[{text:"A blue triangle on an orange background.",correct:!0},{text:"A red cross on a white background.",correct:!1},{text:"A yellow circle on a blue background.",correct:!1},{text:"A red triangle on a white background.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Article 66 of Protocol I sets a blue equilateral triangle on orange; the red cross is the emblem of medical services."},{question:"A team reaches a fire. Which order of priorities fits the principles of the work?",options:[{text:"Property, then the environment, then life.",correct:!1},{text:"Life, then property, then the environment.",correct:!0},{text:"The environment, then life, then property.",correct:!1},{text:"Whatever is nearest the door.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The first principle is that human life comes first; property and the environment follow."},{question:"A rescuer wants to enter a smoke-filled building without protective equipment. Which principle forbids this?",options:[{text:"Impartiality and equality.",correct:!1},{text:"Team safety.",correct:!0},{text:"Protecting personal information.",correct:!1},{text:"Prevention before response.",correct:!1}],explanation:"An injured rescuer becomes another casualty and cannot save anyone."},{question:"You want to know what the law says about safety requirements in a shop. What is the best way?",options:[{text:"Read a social media post that many people have shared.",correct:!1},{text:"Ask the Civil Defense Directorate for the official text and instructions.",correct:!0},{text:"Copy the shop next door, since it is open too.",correct:!1},{text:"Call 122 and ask the ambulance call taker about the law.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Correct legal information comes from the official text and the competent authority; 122 is the ambulance number for medical emergencies."},{question:"Which of these is not in the Article 61 list of tasks?",options:[{text:"Management of shelters.",correct:!1},{text:"Detection of danger areas.",correct:!1},{text:"Collecting taxes from shops.",correct:!0},{text:"Firefighting.",correct:!1}],explanation:"All Article 61 tasks are humanitarian, to protect people; tax collection is not one of them."},{question:'Someone in a uniform like Civil Defense asks people for money "for an inspection." What do you do?',options:[{text:"I pay to avoid trouble and ask him for a receipt.",correct:!1},{text:"I do not pay and I inform the Civil Defense Directorate or the police (104).",correct:!0},{text:"I confront him and pull off the uniform by force in front of everyone.",correct:!1},{text:"I post his photo on social media and do nothing else.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Pretending to be Civil Defense damages public trust; the right action is to inform the official bodies, not to confront. Ask the Directorate about official inspection procedures."}]},{id:2,code:"OPS-ORG-102",icon:"Network",accent:"#2563eb",title:"How a civil defense directorate is organized",subtitle:"The departments, their roles and how they work together",summary:"A civil defense directorate is made up of several departments: some work in the field and others support them. This lesson introduces the departments by name and shows how they work together at one incident. It is an introductory guide, not the official organizational chart.",blocks:[{heading:"Why organization matters",body:"Disasters and incidents come in many kinds: a house fire, a road crash, a flood in a village or a hazardous material at a factory. One team cannot do all of this well. So the directorate is divided into specialist departments, each with its own training, equipment and tasks. Dividing the work is not enough on its own: all departments must work to one plan under one command so that work is not duplicated and nothing is forgotten. The departments in this lesson come from the guide to Civil Defense departments in this program and use the same names."},{heading:"Command and support departments",points:["Directorate & administration: Organizes priorities and resources. Plans preparedness and follows up on the work of teams. Connects field activity with the relevant authorities.","Emergency operations room: Receives incident information and organizes it. Coordinates the dispatch of suitable teams. Follows developments and shares updates with responders.","Prevention, inspection & safety: Reviews fire-prevention and safety arrangements. Identifies deficiencies and records findings. Supports follow-up and safety awareness in buildings and workplaces.","Media & awareness: Explains safety messages to the public. Produces awareness materials and supports community outreach. Shares information in coordination with authorized sources.","Education & training: Organizes learning and practical exercises. Builds preparedness and professional skills. Supports safety education in schools and other learning settings.","Logistics & equipment: Supports vehicle and equipment readiness. Organizes supplies and maintenance needs. Helps field teams obtain the resources needed for their work."]},{heading:"Field departments",points:["Firefighting: Specialist crews respond to fires. They work to limit fire spread and protect people. Their work includes coordination with rescue and support teams.","First aid: Trained responders provide first aid within their qualifications. They coordinate with medical services. They support the transfer of people who need medical care.","Land rescue: Specialist crews help in traffic accidents, collapses and difficult terrain. They assess access and rescue needs. They coordinate safe evacuation with other responders.","Hazardous materials: Specialist teams assess hazardous-material incidents. They control access and coordinate protective measures. They cooperate with the authorities responsible for the substances involved.","Water rescue: Qualified crews assist in water-related emergencies. They use suitable rescue equipment and boats. They coordinate rescue and evacuation during flooding.","Mines & unexploded ordnance: This field is handled by authorized specialists. They assess suspected explosive hazards and secure the area. They coordinate with the competent authorities; this guide does not teach handling or disposal.","Relief & shelter: Supports people affected by emergencies. Coordinates shelter and the distribution of basic supplies. Works with humanitarian and service organizations."]},{heading:"How departments work together at one incident",body:"Imagine a fire in an apartment building. The emergency operations room takes the call and dispatches firefighting and rescue crews. Firefighters put out the fire, rescuers bring out trapped people and first aid teams coordinate with the ambulance service. Logistics supplies water, fuel and equipment. If families are left without a home, relief and shelter steps in. After the incident, prevention, inspection and safety examines the cause of the fire and the safety failings, and media and awareness prepares a lesson for the public. One incident commander directs all of this work."},{heading:"The public and the departments",body:"The public reaches the operations room through 115, and it sends the right department. You do not need to know which department should come: just say clearly what happened and where. For everyday matters, such as questions about safety inspections, training courses or awareness campaigns, follow the Directorate's instructions; the official way to contact each department still needs confirmation. Remember that this guide is not the official organizational chart, and the names and number of departments can differ from one directorate to another."}],steps:[{title:"The call is received",detail:"The emergency operations room receives the information and organizes it.",critical:!0},{title:"The right teams are sent",detail:"According to the type of incident: firefighting, land rescue, hazardous materials or water rescue.",critical:!0},{title:"One commander",detail:"The incident commander assigns tasks and coordinates with the ambulance service and police."},{title:"Support",detail:"Logistics supplies equipment; relief and shelter supports affected people."},{title:"After the incident",detail:"Inspection finds the causes, and media and awareness passes the lessons on to the public."}],warnings:["Do not call staff members' personal numbers about an incident; call 115 so the operations room can send the right team.","This guide is not the official organizational chart; for official roles and powers, follow the Directorate's instructions."],tips:["State the type of incident clearly when you call so the right team is sent.","For questions about inspection and safety requirements, ask the Directorate what the official route is.","If you want to volunteer, education and training is the place to start."],activity:{prompt:"A fishing boat has capsized on a river and two people are in the water. Which department is sent first?",options:[{text:"Water rescue, with first aid.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: it has qualified crews and suitable boats for this."},{text:"Prevention, inspection and safety.",correct:!1,feedback:"This department inspects; it does not perform water rescue."},{text:"Logistics and equipment.",correct:!1,feedback:"Logistics supports, but rescue is the job of trained crews."},{text:"Media and awareness.",correct:!1,feedback:"Awareness matters, but not in the first minutes of a rescue."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"At a factory a sharp smell is coming from a tank and two workers have headaches. Which department does the operations room send?",options:[{text:"Hazardous materials, with first aid and the ambulance service.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the substance must be assessed and people protected."},{text:"Water rescue only.",correct:!1,feedback:"This incident has nothing to do with water."},{text:"Relief and shelter.",correct:!1,feedback:"This department comes later if people are left without a home."}]},{prompt:"The teams have arrived. The factory owner wants to talk to each team separately. What is right?",options:[{text:"He gives his information (substance, location, workers) to the incident commander.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: one commander takes the information and shares it with all teams."},{text:"He goes into the factory himself to shut the tank.",correct:!1,feedback:"That puts him in danger; only specialist teams with protective equipment go in."},{text:"He gives each team different orders.",correct:!1,feedback:"Separate orders cause confusion; orders come from the incident commander."}]}],quiz:[{question:"Which department takes the 115 call and dispatches teams?",options:[{text:"The emergency operations room.",correct:!0},{text:"The education and training department.",correct:!1},{text:"The logistics and equipment department.",correct:!1},{text:"The relief and shelter department.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The operations room receives incident information and coordinates dispatch of the right teams."},{question:"A citizen finds unexploded ordnance in their garden. Which department deals with it and what does the citizen do?",options:[{text:"Mines and unexploded ordnance; they do not touch it, move away and call 115.",correct:!0},{text:"Mines and unexploded ordnance; they carry it somewhere far away so the team can collect it easily.",correct:!1},{text:"Prevention and inspection; they take a close-up photo and send it to friends.",correct:!1},{text:"Logistics; they dig it out themselves with a tool.",correct:!1}],explanation:"This field belongs only to authorized specialists; the citizen does not touch or approach, keeps others away and reports."},{question:"After a market fire, which department examines the cause and the safety failings?",options:[{text:"Land rescue for accidents.",correct:!1},{text:"Prevention, inspection and safety.",correct:!0},{text:"Relief, shelter and support.",correct:!1},{text:"Water rescue and boats.",correct:!1}],explanation:"This department reviews safety arrangements, identifies failings and records findings."},{question:"Why do all departments at an incident work under one commander?",options:[{text:"So that work is not duplicated and nothing is missed.",correct:!0},{text:"So that each department can work as it likes.",correct:!1},{text:"So that fewer teams are at the scene.",correct:!1},{text:"So that the ambulance and police stay away.",correct:!1}],explanation:"One commander and one plan prevent confusion and duplication and make coordination with 122 and 104 easier."},{question:"After a flood, ten families are left homeless. Which department coordinates shelter?",options:[{text:"Media and awareness.",correct:!1},{text:"Hazardous materials.",correct:!1},{text:"Relief and shelter.",correct:!0},{text:"Firefighting.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Relief and shelter coordinates shelter and basic supplies with humanitarian organizations."},{question:"You want to organize an evacuation exercise at your school. Which department is best to ask?",options:[{text:"Education and training.",correct:!0},{text:"The operations room through 115.",correct:!1},{text:"Mines and unexploded ordnance.",correct:!1},{text:"Water rescue.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Education and training organizes exercises and safety education in schools; keep 115 free for emergencies."}]},{id:3,code:"OPS-RSP-103",icon:"PhoneCall",accent:"#ea580c",title:"From the 115 call to the scene: the response cycle",subtitle:"Call, dispatch, arrival, size-up and handover",summary:"Every incident starts with a call and ends with handing over the scene. This lesson explains the stages of the response cycle: what the caller tells the call taker, how the operations room sends teams, what the teams do at the scene and how the scene is handed over.",blocks:[{heading:"Stage one: the call",body:`The caller is the first link in the rescue chain. Correct, short information cuts dispatch time. Give it in this order:

• Location: area, street, a known landmark and floor.
• What happened: fire, crash, collapse, flood or gas.
• How many people are hurt or trapped.
• Dangers: gas, wires, chemicals or a weakened building.
• Your name and phone number.

Do not hang up until the call taker tells you to. If you do not know the address, look for nearby landmarks such as a mosque, a school or a well-known shop. Call 115 for fire and rescue and 122 if people are hurt; call both when there are both.`},{heading:"Stage two: dispatch",body:"The emergency operations room records the information and sends the right teams for the type and size of incident: firefighting, land rescue, hazardous materials or water rescue. When needed, the ambulance service (122) and the police (104) are also informed. The call taker may give you instructions until the teams arrive, such as leaving the building or keeping away from something dangerous. The operations room keeps sharing new information with the teams on the road. The Duhok Directorate's dispatch procedures and standards (which team for which incident, arrival times) have not been officially published, so this lesson gives no figures."},{heading:"Stage three: arrival and size-up",body:"When the team arrives, the first job is to size up the scene before anything else. The incident commander looks at these questions: are lives at risk, and how many? What are the hazards, such as gas, electricity, collapse or chemicals? Which way is the fire or incident spreading? Are the teams and equipment on hand enough, or is backup needed? Based on the answers, the commander sets the plan, assigns tasks and marks a protected zone around the scene. People at the scene, such as the building owner or the caller, give the commander key information: who is still inside, where the gas was shut off and where the entrances are."},{heading:"Stage four: operations, handover and close",body:"Teams work in this order: life, then stopping the spread, then protecting property. Casualties are handed to the ambulance crew with a clear handover: what happened, what was done and when. Once the incident is under control, teams check the scene so the fire does not rekindle and no hidden hazard remains. The scene is then formally handed over to the responsible party, such as the building owner, the police or the municipality, with instructions on when and how people may return. The cycle closes with records, cleaning and readying equipment for the next incident, and a review of causes and lessons learned."},{heading:"The public's role at each stage",body:`The public can make the response cycle faster or slower:

• During the call, stay calm and speak clearly.
• Keep the road clear for emergency vehicles and do not follow them.
• Send someone to the road to guide the team to the place, if it is safe.
• Stay outside the protected zone and do not take photos.
• Give your information to the commander and follow instructions.
• Do not go back to the scene until the responsible party allows it.`}],steps:[{title:"Call",detail:"Location, what happened, how many people, dangers, name and number; do not hang up until told.",critical:!0},{title:"Dispatch",detail:"The operations room sends the right teams and informs 122 and 104 when needed."},{title:"Arrival and size-up",detail:"Lives, hazards, spread and need for backup; then the plan and the protected zone.",critical:!0},{title:"Operations",detail:"Life, then stopping the spread, then property.",critical:!0},{title:"Handover",detail:"Casualties are handed clearly to the ambulance crew; the scene is handed to the responsible party after a check."},{title:"Close",detail:"Records, readying equipment and lessons learned."}],warnings:["Never make a false call to 115: a team sent to a false call may not be available for a real incident elsewhere.","Once the team has arrived, do not enter the protected zone or go back into the building without permission.","Do not follow emergency vehicles or stop on the road to watch."],tips:["Know the landmarks near your home so you can give the location quickly.","If it is safe, send someone to the road to guide the team in.","Have key information ready for the commander (who is inside, gas, keys)."],activity:{prompt:"You see a car crash with one person trapped in a car. How do you start your call to 115?",options:[{text:"With the location: road, direction and a nearby landmark.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: without the location the team cannot get there, so give it first."},{text:"By telling everything you saw from the beginning.",correct:!1,feedback:"A long story wastes time; location first, then a short summary."},{text:"With your name and job.",correct:!1,feedback:"Name and number matter, but they come after the location and incident."},{text:"By asking when the team will arrive.",correct:!1,feedback:"That question gives no information; the team sets off once it knows the location."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:`You call 115 about smoke from a neighbor's house. The call taker says "stay on the line." What do you do?`,options:[{text:"I stay on the line and answer questions from a safe place.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the call taker may have key questions or instructions."},{text:"I hang up to go and look.",correct:!1,feedback:"Hanging up cuts the information and going near the smoke is dangerous."},{text:"I talk to someone else on another phone.",correct:!1,feedback:"This takes your attention away from the call taker's questions."}]},{prompt:"The fire crew has arrived. The homeowner is outside and knows his daughter is on the second floor. What does he do?",options:[{text:"He tells the crew commander at once: who, which floor and which room.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: this is the basis of the size-up and directs the rescue."},{text:"He goes in himself to get her.",correct:!1,feedback:"He may become another casualty; the crew goes in with protective equipment."},{text:"He stays quiet so as not to disturb the crew.",correct:!1,feedback:"Silence hides life-saving information."}]}],quiz:[{question:"In a 115 call, which information comes first?",options:[{text:"The caller's name.",correct:!1},{text:"The location.",correct:!0},{text:"The start time.",correct:!1},{text:"The building color.",correct:!1}],explanation:"If the call drops, the team can still arrive if it has the location, so location comes first."},{question:"The team arrives. What does the commander do before anything else?",options:[{text:"Size-up: lives, hazards, spread and needs.",correct:!0},{text:"Sprays water over the whole building at once.",correct:!1},{text:"Writes a report for the Directorate.",correct:!1},{text:"Talks to journalists about the incident.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The size-up sets the plan and prevents unplanned, dangerous work."},{question:"You see a fire engine with its siren on the road. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I follow it to see where it goes.",correct:!1},{text:"I clear the way safely and do not follow it.",correct:!0},{text:"I stop in the middle of the road and wait.",correct:!1},{text:"I speed up to reach the junction first.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Clearing the way safely shortens arrival time; following blocks the road and adds risk."},{question:"The fire is out. When can the family go back into the house?",options:[{text:"As soon as the flames are gone.",correct:!1},{text:"Once the smoke thins out.",correct:!1},{text:"After a check and official permission.",correct:!0},{text:"Once the teams have packed up and left the street.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Fire can rekindle and the building may be weakened; return only after the check and permission."},{question:"There is a fire and an injured person. Which number do you call?",options:[{text:"Only 104.",correct:!1},{text:"115 and 122.",correct:!0},{text:"112.",correct:!1},{text:"Only 122, the ambulance.",correct:!1}],explanation:"115 is for fire and rescue and 122 for the ambulance; when there are both, both are informed."},{question:"At an incident scene, which action by the public helps?",options:[{text:"Filming close up and posting it on social media.",correct:!1},{text:"Parking near the scene to watch what happens.",correct:!1},{text:"Telling the commander what you know and staying back.",correct:!0},{text:"Going in to bring out belongings.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Information helps the size-up and staying out keeps the way clear; filming and going in add danger."}]},{id:4,code:"OPS-FIR-104",icon:"Flame",accent:"#b91c1c",title:"Firefighting work",subtitle:"How firefighters work and what the public does",summary:"Firefighting is not just spraying water: it also covers rescue, stopping the spread, controlling smoke and checking after the fire. This lesson shows the order in which firefighters work, the equipment they use and how the public can help without putting themselves in danger.",blocks:[{heading:"How fire lives and how it goes out",body:"Fire needs three things: heat, fuel and oxygen, plus a chain reaction that keeps it going. Every way of putting fire out removes one of these: water takes away heat, foam and powder cut off oxygen, shutting a gas valve or moving fuel removes the fuel, and some agents break the chain reaction. This knowledge is the basis of firefighting, because the right agent for one fire can be dangerous on another: water on burning cooking oil causes a violent flare-up, and water on live electrical equipment risks electric shock. So firefighters identify the type of fire first."},{heading:"The order of work at a fire",body:`Fire crews work in a clear order:

• Size-up: who is inside, where the fire is and what the hazards are.
• Rescue: trapped people are the first task.
• Protecting nearby buildings so the fire does not spread.
• Confining the fire where it is, then putting it out.
• Controlling smoke and heat in a coordinated way, so fresh air does not feed the fire.
• Overhaul: searching walls and ceilings for hot spots and hidden fire.
• Protecting property from water and smoke, then handing over the scene.`},{heading:"Equipment and firefighter protection",body:"Firefighters wear special protective clothing, helmets, gloves and heat-resistant boots, and inside smoke they use self-contained breathing apparatus (SCBA), because fire smoke is full of toxic gases and you cannot see in it. Fire engines carry water, pumps, hoses, ladders and rescue equipment. In cities, firefighters rely on fire hydrants and clear roads. Firefighters never go into a smoke-filled building alone; they work in teams of two or more and the commander knows where each one is. This equipment and training show why an ordinary person should not go into a burning building: without it, smoke can knock a person unconscious in a few breaths."},{heading:"Special fires: gas, electricity and cooking oil",body:"Some fires need special procedures. At a gas fire the first job is stopping the gas: a qualified person closes the valve only if it is safe to reach. A gas flame that cannot be stopped is not put out, because unburned gas builds up and can explode; then everyone gets out and calls 115. At an electrical fire, the power is cut in a safe way and water is not sprayed on live equipment. At a cooking oil fire, water is never used; a metal lid or a special extinguisher (Class K) is used and the heat is turned off if it is safe. When in doubt, firefighters take the plan from the commander."},{heading:"The public's role in a fire",body:`The best help the public can give firefighters:

• Get out, warn everyone and call 115 from outside.
• Close doors behind you as you leave to slow the fire and smoke.
• Use an extinguisher only if the fire is small and just starting, your exit is behind you, you are trained and 115 has been called; if it does not go out at once, get out.
• Stay outside and do not go back for belongings.
• Keep hydrants and roads clear and do not park in front of building entrances.
• Give firefighters information: who is still inside and where gas or cylinders are.`}],steps:[{title:"Size-up",detail:"Who is inside, where the fire is, the type of fire and the hazards (gas, electricity, chemicals).",critical:!0},{title:"Rescue",detail:"Trapped people come first; crews work in pairs with breathing apparatus.",critical:!0},{title:"Stop the spread",detail:"Protect nearby buildings and confine the fire."},{title:"Extinguish",detail:"The right agent for the type of fire; gas is stopped first if it is safe.",critical:!0},{title:"Overhaul",detail:"Search for hot spots and hidden fire so it does not rekindle."}],warnings:["Never throw water on burning cooking oil: the fire flares up violently and can burn you.","Do not put out a gas flame that cannot be stopped: unburned gas builds up and can explode.","Never go back into a burning building for belongings: smoke can knock you out in a few breaths."],tips:["Close doors behind you as you leave.","Keep hydrants near your home clear and do not park in front of them.","Know where the gas valve and main electrical switch are in your home so you can tell firefighters."],activity:{prompt:"A fire starts in a neighbor's kitchen and a pot of oil is burning. The neighbor wants to pour water on it. What do you say?",options:[{text:"No water; if it is safe put the lid on and turn off the heat, otherwise get out and call 115.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the lid cuts off oxygen; water makes the fire flare up."},{text:"Pour water quickly before the fire grows.",correct:!1,feedback:"Water on burning oil turns to steam and throws burning oil around."},{text:"Carry the pot outside.",correct:!1,feedback:"Hot oil can spill and burn you and others."},{text:"Open the window to let the smoke out.",correct:!1,feedback:"Fresh air feeds the fire."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"A fire starts in a room of a building and smoke reaches the corridor. You are on the ground floor. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I get out, warn everyone and call 115 from outside.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: smoke is in the corridor, so an extinguisher is not an option."},{text:"I go into the smoky corridor with an extinguisher.",correct:!1,feedback:"Smoke blocks the way out; this fails the go/no-go check."},{text:"I stay in my room and wait without reporting.",correct:!1,feedback:"While leaving is still possible, get out and report."}]},{prompt:"The crew arrives and the commander asks if anyone is still inside. You know an elderly woman lives on the third floor and has not come out. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I tell the commander at once: third floor, which side, and that she cannot move quickly.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: this information directs the rescue."},{text:"I run up the stairs myself.",correct:!1,feedback:"Without breathing apparatus you become another casualty."},{text:"I say nothing because I am not completely sure.",correct:!1,feedback:"Share it even if unsure, and say so; the commander decides."}]}],quiz:[{question:"What do firefighters do first at a fire?",options:[{text:"Size-up and rescue of trapped people.",correct:!0},{text:"Protecting valuables from water.",correct:!1},{text:"Investigating the cause.",correct:!1},{text:"Breaking all windows for the smoke.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Life comes first: the size-up shows who is inside, then rescue follows; property and cause come later."},{question:"A gas appliance is on fire and the valve is inside the flames. What is right?",options:[{text:"Put out the flame with an extinguisher, then close the valve.",correct:!1},{text:"Close the valve quickly with a wet hand.",correct:!1},{text:"Get out, get everyone out and call 115 from outside.",correct:!0},{text:"Spray water on the flame to cool it.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The valve is closed only if it is safe to reach; a gas flame that cannot be stopped is not put out because of the explosion risk."},{question:"Why do firefighters enter smoke with SCBA?",options:[{text:"Because smoke contains toxic gases and little oxygen.",correct:!0},{text:"Because it makes their clothing lighter.",correct:!1},{text:"Because it pushes heat away from the fire.",correct:!1},{text:"Because it shows the way out.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Toxic gases in smoke can knock a person out in a few breaths; SCBA supplies clean air."},{question:"Why do firefighters open walls and ceilings after the fire is out?",options:[{text:"To find hidden fire and hot spots.",correct:!0},{text:"To increase the damage for insurance.",correct:!1},{text:"To empty the building faster.",correct:!1},{text:"To test their equipment.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Fire can smolder inside walls and flare up later; overhaul prevents this."},{question:"You have an extinguisher and a small fire starts in a wastebasket. Which condition must hold before you use it?",options:[{text:"The fire is small, the exit is behind you and 115 has been called.",correct:!0},{text:"You are alone so nobody gets in your way.",correct:!1},{text:"Black smoke has filled the room.",correct:!1},{text:"The windows and doors are open so plenty of fresh air comes in.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The go/no-go check: a small, new fire, the right extinguisher, the exit behind you, others warned and 115 called, training and no smoke blocking the way; if any is missing, get out."},{question:"Which action by the public slows firefighters down?",options:[{text:"Closing doors on the way out.",correct:!1},{text:"Parking in front of a fire hydrant.",correct:!0},{text:"Telling the crew where gas cylinders are.",correct:!1},{text:"Staying outside in a safe place.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Firefighters rely on hydrants; a car in front of one wastes vital time. The other actions help."}]},{id:5,code:"OPS-RES-105",icon:"LifeBuoy",accent:"#0891b2",title:"Rescue work: collapse, road, water, height and mountain",subtitle:"How rescuers work and what the public must not do",summary:"Rescue is specialist work that needs training and special equipment. This lesson shows five kinds of rescue, explains the principles they share and sets out how the public can help without becoming new victims.",blocks:[{heading:"Principles shared by every rescue",body:'Whatever the kind of rescue, rescuers work on the same principles. First, rescuer safety, because an injured rescuer becomes another casualty and takes the team out of action. Second, size up the scene before going close: is the building still moving, is the current strong, is there electricity or gas? Third, reach the person, reassure them and protect them from further harm, then get them out. Fourth, hand them over to first aid and the ambulance service. Rescuers follow the rule "from least risk to most": if they can rescue by talking or by reaching with something, they do not put themselves in danger. This rule applies to the public too.'},{heading:"Collapse rescue",body:"After an earthquake, a gas explosion or a building failure, people can be trapped under walls and ceilings. Land rescue teams first assess the building and mark the dangerous areas, then search for trapped people by listening, calling out and using special equipment. They brace walls and ceilings (shoring) before going under them, and they work slowly so that more debris does not fall. In large collapses, international guidelines (INSARAG) set out how search and rescue is organized. The public can give information: who was in the building and in which room. But entering a collapsed building or lifting rubble by hand can cause a second collapse."},{heading:"Road and water rescue",body:'At road crashes, rescuers first protect the scene from other traffic, turn off the engine and check for fire and fuel hazards. Then they open doors and the roof with hydraulic tools to get the trapped person out without moving the neck and back. In water, rescue follows the order "talk, reach, throw, go by boat"; swimming out to someone is the last resort and only for trained teams. Water rescue teams use rescue suits, ropes and suitable boats, because floodwater is strong, cold and hides objects. Even shallow moving water can knock a person down and carry a car away.'},{heading:"Height and mountain rescue",body:"Some people get stuck in high places: on a roof, on a crane, in a well or on a mountain slope. Rescue at height needs ropes, harnesses, anchoring devices and special training, because any mistake can cause a fall. In the mountains, rescuers may need a long time to arrive, the weather can change quickly and the night is darker and colder. So people going into the mountains should tell someone their plan, carry water, warm clothes and a charged phone, and stay on known paths. If someone is lost or injured, stay where you are if it is safe, describe your location clearly and signal so rescuers can see you."},{heading:"The public's role during a rescue",body:`The public can save lives or add to the danger:

• Call 115 and describe the location clearly; call 122 too if people are hurt.
• Do not go into the water to rescue unless you are trained; reach with something or throw something that floats.
• Do not enter a collapsed building or lift rubble by hand.
• Do not move a crash victim if the place is safe, especially if a neck or back injury is possible.
• Keep the road clear for the teams and stay outside the work area.
• Give rescuers your information: who was where and when.`}],steps:[{title:"Rescuer safety",detail:"Check the hazards before going close: collapse, water, electricity, gas and traffic.",critical:!0},{title:"Size-up",detail:"How many people, where and in what condition; which teams and equipment are needed.",critical:!0},{title:"Reach",detail:"From least risk: talk, reach, throw, then go with equipment.",critical:!0},{title:"Protect and extricate",detail:"Reassure, protect the neck and back when needed, and extricate with suitable equipment."},{title:"Handover",detail:"Hand over to first aid and the ambulance service (122) with a summary of the incident."}],warnings:["Do not go into the water to save someone unless trained: many untrained rescuers drown.","Do not enter a collapsed building or lift rubble by hand: a second collapse can kill you and the trapped person.","Do not pull a crash victim out of a car if the place is safe: moving them can worsen a neck or back injury."],tips:["When you go into the mountains, tell someone your plan and carry a charged phone.","Near water, keep something that floats or a rope ready.","If you are trapped, tap on a pipe or wall so rescuers can hear you."],activity:{prompt:"A child falls into a fast river. You have no water rescue training. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I shout for help, call 115 and throw something that floats or a rope.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: throwing reaches the child without you entering the water."},{text:"I jump in at once.",correct:!1,feedback:"A strong current carries off untrained rescuers too; there would be two victims."},{text:"I run along the bank after the child without calling.",correct:!1,feedback:"Without a call no team comes; call and throw together."},{text:"I film it to show the rescuers later.",correct:!1,feedback:"Filming wastes vital time."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"On a road outside town a car has overturned; the driver is conscious inside and says his neck hurts. No fuel is leaking and the car is still. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I protect the scene from traffic, call 115 and 122 and talk to him without moving him.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the scene is safe, so moving him is not needed and could harm his neck."},{text:"I pull him out quickly.",correct:!1,feedback:"When the scene is safe, pulling him out can worsen a neck injury."},{text:"I give him food and water for strength.",correct:!1,feedback:"That does not help his neck and may cause problems later at hospital; calling and reassurance come first."}]},{prompt:"A little later there is a smell of fuel and light smoke from the engine. The team has not arrived. What changes?",options:[{text:"The scene is no longer safe; if I can do it safely, I move him away gently, protecting the neck and back, and update 115.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: moving is only for an unsafe scene, protecting the neck and back."},{text:"Nothing changes; I still will not move him.",correct:!1,feedback:"The fire risk is new; leaving him may endanger his life."},{text:"I pour water on the engine.",correct:!1,feedback:"Water on a fuel fire can spread it and brings you close to danger."}]}],quiz:[{question:"Which order is right in water rescue?",options:[{text:"Swim, then throw, then talk.",correct:!1},{text:"Talk, reach, throw, then boat.",correct:!0},{text:"Boat, then swim, then talk.",correct:!1},{text:"Throw, then swim, then reach.",correct:!1}],explanation:"From least risk to most; swimming is the last resort and only for trained teams."},{question:"Why do rescue teams shore walls before going under them?",options:[{text:"To prevent a second collapse.",correct:!0},{text:"To make the building look better afterwards.",correct:!1},{text:"To make trapped people louder.",correct:!1},{text:"To finish the job faster.",correct:!1}],explanation:"A collapsed building may still move; shoring protects rescuer and victim."},{question:"Going into the mountains, which preparation makes rescue easier if you get lost?",options:[{text:"Telling someone your plan and carrying a charged phone.",correct:!0},{text:"Going alone on a new path nobody knows.",correct:!1},{text:"Setting out in the evening to avoid the heat.",correct:!1},{text:"Carrying as little water as possible to keep the load light.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Someone who knows your plan raises the alarm if you do not return, and rescuers know where to look."},{question:"A building has collapsed and you hear a voice under the rubble. What is best?",options:[{text:"I lift the rubble by hand until I reach them.",correct:!1},{text:"I call 115 and point out the spot to the team.",correct:!0},{text:"I drive onto the rubble to see better.",correct:!1},{text:"I gather people to lift the rubble together.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Lifting rubble without shoring or a plan can cause a second collapse; pointing out the spot to the team is best."},{question:"A road is covered by shallow, moving floodwater and you are driving. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I cross quickly before it rises.",correct:!1},{text:"I follow a big vehicle across.",correct:!1},{text:"I turn back and find another way.",correct:!0},{text:"I stop in the water until it drops.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Shallow moving water can carry a car away and its depth is hidden; turning back is safest."},{question:"Which principle comes first in every kind of rescue?",options:[{text:"Speed at any cost.",correct:!1},{text:"Rescuer safety.",correct:!0},{text:"Protecting the car and property.",correct:!1},{text:"Taking photos for the report.",correct:!1}],explanation:"An injured rescuer saves no one and takes the team out of action."}]},{id:6,code:"OPS-MED-106",icon:"Ambulance",accent:"#0284c7",title:"Medical support and cooperation with the ambulance service",subtitle:"First aid at the scene and handing casualties to the ambulance",summary:"Civil Defense and the ambulance service (122) are two links in one chain. Rescuers reach the casualty and give first aid within their qualifications; ambulance crews continue medical care and take the casualty to hospital. This lesson shows how this cooperation works.",blocks:[{heading:"Civil Defense's medical role",body:"Protocol I to the Geneva Conventions lists medical services and first aid among civil defense tasks. According to the department guide, trained responders give first aid within their qualifications, coordinate with medical services and support the transfer of people who need care. This means rescuers may be the first to reach a casualty under rubble, inside an overturned car or in the water, before the ambulance can get close. Their job then is to keep the casualty alive, protect them from further harm and get them out safely. Advanced medical treatment is the work of ambulance crews and the hospital."},{heading:"Life-saving first aid at the scene",body:`At the scene, rescuers and trained citizens focus on a few life-saving actions:

• Severe bleeding: direct pressure on the wound, and a tourniquet early for life-threatening limb bleeding.
• Cardiac arrest: CPR and an AED if available; untrained people give Hands-Only CPR.
• Unconscious and breathing normally: the recovery position only if there is no suspected injury to the neck, back, hip or pelvis.
• Burns: cool running water for 20 minutes.
• Protection from cold and reassurance.

Nothing is given by mouth to an unconscious person and no medicines are given, except helping a person use their own prescribed device.`},{heading:"Handing a casualty to the ambulance",body:"When the ambulance arrives, a short, clear handover saves time and reduces mistakes. Rescuers report in this order: what happened and how (a fall from height, trapped in a car, a long time under rubble); the injuries seen; key signs such as consciousness, breathing and bleeding; what was done, such as pressure or a tourniquet, and the time the tourniquet was applied; and how the condition has changed since arrival. This information then goes with the casualty to hospital. If there are several casualties, the commander and the ambulance crew decide the order of transport by how serious the injury is, not by who shouts loudest. Local handover procedures still need official confirmation."},{heading:"Coordination at the scene",body:"At large incidents, Civil Defense and the ambulance service work to one plan. Rescuers work in the danger zone and bring casualties out to a safe place outside where ambulance crews work. Ambulance access and exit routes must stay clear, and the commander decides where ambulances park. If hazardous materials are involved, casualties are decontaminated before handover so that ambulance and hospital staff are not harmed. The operations room stays in contact with medical services so hospitals can prepare to receive many casualties. Joint exercises make this coordination better."},{heading:"The public's role",body:`The public can be the first link:

• Call 122 for injured and sick people, and 115 too if there is fire, entrapment or collapse.
• If you are trained, give first aid; if not, follow the call taker's instructions.
• Do not move a casualty if the place is safe.
• Do not give any medicine and give nothing by mouth to an unconscious person.
• Keep the road clear for the ambulance and do not crowd around the casualty.
• Tell the ambulance crew what you saw.`}],steps:[{title:"Scene safety",detail:"Before approaching the casualty, check for hazards and call 122 and 115.",critical:!0},{title:"Life-saving care",detail:"Stop severe bleeding with pressure; if there is no breathing, start CPR.",critical:!0},{title:"Protect",detail:"Do not move the casualty if the place is safe; protect from cold and reassure."},{title:"Handover",detail:"What happened, injuries, signs, what was done and when, and changes in condition.",critical:!0},{title:"Clear route",detail:"Keep the ambulance route in and out clear."}],warnings:["Give nothing by mouth to an unconscious person: they may choke.","Give no medicine except helping a person use their own prescribed device: the wrong medicine can make things worse.","Do not use the recovery position when a neck or back injury is suspected."],tips:["Take a first aid course so you can help while waiting for the ambulance.","If a tourniquet is applied, note the time and tell the ambulance crew.","Send someone to the road to guide the ambulance in."],activity:{prompt:"After a car crash, a person lies unconscious on the road and is breathing normally. The car hit them hard. What do you do until the ambulance arrives?",options:[{text:"I protect the scene from traffic, do not move them, watch their breathing and call 122.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: a hard impact raises suspicion of neck and back injury, so the recovery position is not used."},{text:"I roll them into the recovery position.",correct:!1,feedback:"The recovery position is not used when a neck or back injury is suspected."},{text:"I give them water to wake them up.",correct:!1,feedback:"An unconscious person may choke on water."},{text:"I drive them to hospital in my car.",correct:!1,feedback:"Transport without neck and back protection or medical care can worsen the injury."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"Rescuers bring a worker out from under a collapsed wall. His thigh is bleeding heavily. The ambulance is still on the way. What comes first?",options:[{text:"Direct pressure on the wound, and a tourniquet if the bleeding does not stop.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: severe bleeding kills quickly and comes first."},{text:"Raising his leg and waiting.",correct:!1,feedback:"Raising does not stop bleeding; pressure and a tourniquet are right."},{text:"Giving him a painkiller.",correct:!1,feedback:"Medicine is not the rescuers' job and does not stop bleeding."}]},{prompt:"The tourniquet is on and the ambulance arrives. What do they tell the crew?",options:[{text:"A summary of the incident, the injury, the tourniquet time and changes in condition.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: the tourniquet time is very important to the hospital."},{text:"Only the worker's name.",correct:!1,feedback:"A name is not enough; medical information is needed."},{text:"They loosen the tourniquet to see if the bleeding has stopped.",correct:!1,feedback:"Rescuers never loosen a tourniquet; that is a medical decision."}]}],quiz:[{question:"Someone is unconscious and not breathing. An untrained bystander is there. What is the best thing to do?",options:[{text:"Call 122 and give Hands-Only CPR.",correct:!0},{text:"Splash water on the face and wait.",correct:!1},{text:"Roll them on their side and leave them.",correct:!1},{text:"Wait for the ambulance doing nothing.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Under AHA 2025, untrained bystanders give Hands-Only CPR; every minute without compressions lowers the chance of survival."},{question:"There are several casualties. Who goes first by ambulance?",options:[{text:"Whoever shouts loudest.",correct:!1},{text:"The most seriously injured, as the team judges.",correct:!0},{text:"Whoever the team brought out from the rubble first.",correct:!1},{text:"The one whose relatives are present.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Order follows how serious the injury is; the quiet one may be the worst."},{question:"Why is the tourniquet time recorded?",options:[{text:"So doctors know how long it has been on.",correct:!0},{text:"So rescuers know when to loosen it.",correct:!1},{text:"To write the service bill.",correct:!1},{text:"Because a law requires it on paper.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The tourniquet time matters for medical decisions; rescuers never loosen it."},{question:"At a hazardous materials incident, why are casualties decontaminated before handover?",options:[{text:"So ambulance and hospital staff are not harmed.",correct:!0},{text:"To take better photos for the report.",correct:!1},{text:"To keep their clothes for the family.",correct:!1},{text:"To keep the ambulance looking clean.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The substance can pass from the casualty to staff and the hospital; decontamination prevents this."},{question:"A person with a heart condition says they have their medicine with them. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I give them a different medicine from my bag.",correct:!1},{text:"I call 122 and only help them use their own prescribed medicine.",correct:!0},{text:"I give a double dose so it works faster.",correct:!1},{text:"I take their medicine away and keep it until a doctor or the ambulance arrives.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Help is only with the person's own prescribed device or medicine; we give no other medicine."},{question:"The ambulance reaches a narrow street and people are crowding the casualty. What helps?",options:[{text:"Clearing the way and stepping back.",correct:!0},{text:"Filming so the family will know.",correct:!1},{text:"Carrying the casualty to the ambulance.",correct:!1},{text:"Parking cars on both sides of the street.",correct:!1}],explanation:"A clear route and working space save the crew time; bystanders carrying the casualty can worsen the injury."}]},{id:7,code:"OPS-EVA-107",icon:"Megaphone",accent:"#7c3aed",title:"Warning, shelter and evacuation of the population",subtitle:"When to stay, when to leave and how to prepare",summary:"Warning, evacuation and managing shelters are the first three tasks on the international list of civil defense tasks. This lesson explains how warnings reach people, the difference between staying in place and evacuating, and how families prepare.",blocks:[{heading:"Warning: where it comes from and how",body:"A good warning gives people time to act before the danger arrives. Warnings can reach people by sirens, radio and television, phone messages, loudspeakers on vehicles or door to door. A proper warning says: what the danger is, which area, what to do and for how long. The warning channels and levels used in Duhok Governorate, such as siren sounds and their meanings, have not yet been officially confirmed, so this lesson teaches no specific level or sound. The rule is: listen to official warnings from Civil Defense and the competent authority, and do not believe social media rumors before they are confirmed."},{heading:"Stay in place or evacuate?",body:`There are two main decisions, and the competent authority chooses between them based on the hazard:

• Stay in place (shelter indoors): when going outside is more dangerous than staying, as in some gas or hazardous material releases outdoors. People go to an inner room, close doors and windows and follow instructions.
• Evacuate: when staying is dangerous, as with floods, large fires or a weakened building. People move along set routes to a safe place or a shelter.

The decision is not yours alone when instructions are given: follow what the competent authority says. But if fire or water reaches you, protect your life and get out.`},{heading:"How evacuation works",body:"An organized evacuation starts with a plan: the danger area, exit routes, assembly points and shelter locations. Civil Defense teams and the police manage traffic; people who need help, such as older people, people with disabilities, the sick and unaccompanied children, are brought out first; and people are counted at the assembly point so no one is missing. People should use the set routes, not unknown shortcuts that may have water or fire on them. After an evacuation, return happens only after an official announcement that the area is safe, because buildings, power lines and gas pipes may be damaged."},{heading:"Shelter",body:"A shelter is a safe place where evacuated people stay for a short or long time, such as a school, a hall or a camp. Managing shelters is a civil defense task, and the relief and shelter department coordinates it with humanitarian organizations and service bodies. International standards such as the Sphere Handbook call for clean water, a secure place, health care, protection of women and children and respect for human dignity. In a shelter, people follow staff instructions, keep their space clean, do not light fires indoors and report any illness or hazard. Official shelter locations in Duhok still need confirmation."},{heading:"Family preparedness and the public's role",body:`A prepared family acts quickly and calmly:

• Have a family plan: an assembly point, a contact person outside the area and exit routes.
• Prepare an emergency bag: water, food that does not spoil, prescribed medicines, a flashlight, batteries, important documents and warm clothes.
• Know your older neighbors and people with disabilities and check on them.
• When a warning comes, listen to official instructions and act quickly.
• Do not block emergency vehicles, and if you are told to leave, leave at once.
• Do not spread rumors; share only official information.`}],steps:[{title:"Receive the warning",detail:"Confirm through official channels: what the danger is, which area and what to do.",critical:!0},{title:"Follow the decision",detail:"Stay in place or evacuate, as the competent authority instructs.",critical:!0},{title:"Help those in need",detail:"Warn and bring out older people, people with disabilities and children."},{title:"Use the set route",detail:"Go to the assembly point or shelter and get counted."},{title:"Return with permission",detail:"Return only after an official announcement that the area is safe.",critical:!0}],warnings:["Do not spread social media rumors: a false warning can cause panic and jammed roads.","Do not return after an evacuation without an official announcement: buildings, power lines and gas pipes may be damaged.","Do not light fires inside a shelter: there is a risk of fire and toxic gas."],tips:["Keep the emergency bag somewhere easy to reach near the door.","Practice the family assembly point with every member.","Make copies of important documents and keep them safe."],activity:{prompt:"An official warning says a hazardous material has leaked from a nearby factory and people must stay indoors. Your family wants to leave by car. What do you say?",options:[{text:"We stay inside, close doors and windows and follow instructions.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: with the substance outside, leaving can put us in contaminated air."},{text:"We drive away quickly.",correct:!1,feedback:"This goes against the official instruction and may drive you into the cloud."},{text:"We open the windows for air.",correct:!1,feedback:"Opening windows lets contaminated air in."},{text:"We go to the factory to see what happened.",correct:!1,feedback:"That takes you towards the source of danger."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"The river is rising fast and an official warning orders your neighborhood evacuated. You have an emergency bag, and an elderly neighbor lives alone. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"I get my family ready, alert the neighbor and take him with us.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: people who need help are brought out first."},{text:"I wait to see if the water reaches the house.",correct:!1,feedback:"Waiting cuts the time to leave and roads may be cut off."},{text:"I leave alone and leave the neighbor to the team.",correct:!1,feedback:"The team may be late; alerting the neighbor protects his life."}]},{prompt:"On the set route, water has covered a nearby shortcut and people are crossing it. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stay on the set route and keep out of the water.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: moving water can carry away people and cars."},{text:"I follow the others because they got across.",correct:!1,feedback:"Others getting across does not mean it is safe; the water may rise."},{text:"I stop and take photos.",correct:!1,feedback:"Stopping blocks the way for others and wastes your time."}]}],quiz:[{question:'An official warning says "stay indoors." Which action is right?',options:[{text:"Closing doors and windows and following instructions.",correct:!0},{text:"Going to the market to buy food and water before the shops close.",correct:!1},{text:"Opening windows for fresh air.",correct:!1},{text:"Going to see the incident site.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Staying in place means closing doors and windows and staying in until instructions change."},{question:"During an evacuation, who is brought out first?",options:[{text:"Owners of big vehicles, so they can clear the road for others.",correct:!1},{text:"Those who need help: older people, the sick and unaccompanied children.",correct:!0},{text:"Whoever reaches the assembly point first from the whole area.",correct:!1},{text:"Shop workers, so they can protect goods from theft.",correct:!1}],explanation:"People who cannot leave quickly on their own need more time and help."},{question:'You see a post saying "the dam will break, everyone run." What do you do?',options:[{text:"I forward it to all my friends at once.",correct:!1},{text:"I check official channels before doing anything.",correct:!0},{text:"I drive off without knowing where to.",correct:!1},{text:"I ask 115 to send the post to everyone.",correct:!1}],explanation:"False rumors cause panic and jams; follow only official warnings."},{question:"After an evacuation a family wants to go home. When is that right?",options:[{text:"When the water has left the street.",correct:!1},{text:"When the neighbors go back and say everything is fine.",correct:!1},{text:"After an official all-clear for the area.",correct:!0},{text:"After one night in the shelter.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Buildings, power and gas may be damaged; only an official announcement confirms safety."},{question:"What belongs in an emergency bag?",options:[{text:"Water, prescribed medicines, a flashlight and documents.",correct:!0},{text:"Large, heavy electronics to keep them safe.",correct:!1},{text:"A gas cylinder for cooking in the shelter.",correct:!1},{text:"All the family's clothes for every season.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The bag should be light and hold essentials; fire and gas are dangerous in a shelter."},{question:"When is evacuation better than staying in place?",options:[{text:"When a hazardous material is in the outside air.",correct:!1},{text:"When a flood or large fire is approaching the home.",correct:!0},{text:"Whenever you simply feel like leaving.",correct:!1},{text:"When the warning says stay indoors.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Evacuate when staying is dangerous, as with floods and large fires; for airborne hazards it is usually staying indoors, as instructed."}]},{id:8,code:"OPS-HAZ-108",icon:"Biohazard",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Hazardous materials and CBRN work",subtitle:"Protective actions for the public and the team",summary:"Fuels, gases and chemicals are part of daily life, and a leak or release can harm many people at once. In this lesson you learn how Civil Defense works at a hazardous materials incident and what protective actions protect the public.",blocks:[{heading:"What hazardous materials and CBRN mean",body:"Hazardous materials (HazMat) are substances that can harm people, property or the environment if they escape from their container: fuels, cooking and industrial gases, chemicals in factories and farms, and materials carried by road. CBRN stands for chemical, biological, radiological and nuclear hazards; it covers rare but serious events that may affect a whole area. Protocol I lists the detection and marking of danger areas and decontamination among the tasks of civil defense. This lesson does not teach the properties of specific agents. It teaches the protective actions that keep the public and the team safe, because the first minutes depend on simple and correct behavior."},{heading:"Signs of a hazardous materials incident",body:`Many releases are noticed by ordinary people before any team arrives. The signs include:

• A cloud, vapor or mist that should not be there.
• An unusual or sharp smell, or a hissing sound from a tank or pipe.
• A damaged tanker or container, especially one carrying warning labels or colored plates.
• Several people with the same complaints at the same time, such as coughing, burning eyes or difficulty breathing.
• Dead birds or animals with no clear cause.

When you notice these signs, do not go closer to read a label or to see what happened. Move away and call 115.`},{heading:"Protective actions for the public",body:"Two protective actions save lives: evacuation and shelter in place. The competent authority decides which one fits, and you follow its instructions. Until then, move away from the release toward the side the wind is coming from and, if you can, to higher ground, and never walk through a cloud, spill or smoke. If you are told to shelter in place, go inside, close windows and doors, switch off fans and air conditioning and listen for official information. In a radiation emergency the advice is the same: get inside, stay inside and stay tuned. Community lesson 21 explains these actions in detail for families."},{heading:"How Civil Defense works at the scene",body:"The team arrives from upwind and first isolates the area: it keeps people out and moves those nearby to safety. The scene is divided into zones. The hot zone around the release is entered only by trained members with the right protective equipment. In the warm zone people and equipment are decontaminated, and the cold zone holds command, the ambulance service and support. The team identifies the material from labels, transport documents, the driver or the site staff, and asks specialists for advice. The incident commander coordinates with the ambulance service (122), the police (104), health and environment bodies, and decides with the competent authority on evacuation or shelter in place."},{heading:"Decontamination and after the incident",body:"A person who was close to the release may carry the material on clothes and skin and can spread it to others, including family and hospital staff. Follow the team's instructions. The general advice is to remove outer clothing carefully, place it in a bag, wash skin and hair with soap and water, and avoid touching the face and eyes. Tell the team and the ambulance service that you were near the release. After the response, the competent bodies clean up the site and monitor the area; people return only when the authority declares it safe. Food or water that may have been reached by the material is not used until officials say it is safe."}],steps:[{title:"Recognize from a distance",detail:"Notice the signs (cloud, smell, damaged tanker, people with the same complaints) without going closer.",critical:!0},{title:"Isolate the area",detail:"Keep people out, move those nearby away upwind and uphill, and call 115 with the location and what you see.",critical:!0},{title:"Identify the material",detail:"The team reads labels and documents from a safe distance and asks the driver, the site staff or specialists."},{title:"Decide the protective action",detail:"With the competent authority, order evacuation or shelter in place and tell the public clearly.",critical:!0},{title:"Decontaminate and treat",detail:"People who were exposed are decontaminated in the warm zone and handed to the ambulance service.",critical:!0},{title:"Clean up and return",detail:"The competent bodies clean up and monitor; people return only when the authority declares the area safe."}],warnings:["Do not walk into a cloud, spill or smoke to help someone: you will become another casualty; call 115 and guide the team.","Do not touch, smell or move a leaking container, a suspicious powder or an unknown package to find out what it is.","Do not leave a shelter-in-place order to fetch a car or belongings: wait for the official instruction."],tips:["When you report a release, say what you see and the direction of the wind if you know it.","At work, know where the safety data sheets (SDS) are kept and who is responsible for the site plan.","Keep a phone charged and a radio at home so that you can follow official instructions.","Agree with your family where to meet if you are told to evacuate."],activity:{prompt:"A tanker has overturned on a road near your home. A liquid is spreading and there is a sharp smell. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Go closer to read the label on the tanker so you can tell 115 exactly what it is.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: going closer exposes you; the team identifies the material from a safe distance."},{text:"Spray the liquid with a garden hose to dilute it.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: water can spread some materials or react with them; this is work for the trained team."},{text:"Move away upwind and uphill, keep others back and call 115 with the location and what you see.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: distance and an early, clear report are the most important protective actions."},{text:"Film the scene from close up to share it as a warning.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: staying close to film puts your life at risk and may spread fear."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"A cloud from a factory leak is drifting toward a residential area. The authority has not yet given an order and the wind is blowing the cloud toward the homes. What is the best advice for the residents?",options:[{text:"Everyone should drive out at once on the road that passes the factory.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: that road may pass through the cloud; leaving without instructions can be more dangerous."},{text:"Stay away from the cloud, go inside if it is near, close windows and doors and follow official instructions.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: until an order is given, staying out of the cloud and sheltering inside protects people."},{text:"Go up on the roof to see where the cloud is going.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: being outside exposes people to the cloud."}]},{prompt:"The authority orders shelter in place. A neighbor who was near the factory arrives with burning eyes and a cough. What should be done?",options:[{text:"Bring the neighbor inside, have them remove outer clothing into a bag and wash with soap and water, and call 122 and say they were near the leak.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: this reduces exposure and tells the ambulance service what they will face."},{text:"Send the neighbor to walk to the hospital alone.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: walking outside continues the exposure and may spread the material."},{text:"Rub the neighbor's eyes with a cloth to clean them.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: rubbing can spread the material; wash and follow the instructions of the medical team."},{text:"Wait until the neighbor feels better before calling anyone.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: an exposed person needs medical assessment early."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: a delivery truck carrying chemical containers collided with a car near a school. A teacher saw a mist rising from the truck and noticed a sharp smell. She did not go closer. She kept the students inside, closed the windows and switched off the fans, then called 115 with the location and the direction of the wind. The team arrived from upwind, closed the road and set up zones. Two passers-by who had been close were decontaminated and handed to the ambulance service. The students went home only after the authority declared the area safe.",quiz:[{question:"You see a cloud coming from a damaged tanker. In which direction should you move?",options:[{text:"Downwind, so the wind pushes you away faster.",correct:!1},{text:"Upwind and, if possible, to higher ground.",correct:!0},{text:"Toward the tanker to warn the driver.",correct:!1},{text:"Into the nearest basement, to hide there until the cloud has passed.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Moving to the side the wind comes from and to higher ground keeps you out of the cloud; some gases collect in low places."},{question:"The authority orders shelter in place. Which action is wrong?",options:[{text:"Closing windows and doors.",correct:!1},{text:"Switching off fans and air conditioning.",correct:!1},{text:"Opening windows to let fresh air in.",correct:!0},{text:"Listening carefully for official information on the radio or phone.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Opening windows lets the outside air and the material in; shelter in place means sealing the room."},{question:"Who enters the hot zone at a hazardous materials incident?",options:[{text:"Only trained members with the right protective equipment.",correct:!0},{text:"Any volunteer from the area who wants to help the injured quickly.",correct:!1},{text:"Journalists covering the incident.",correct:!1},{text:"Relatives of the injured.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The hot zone is the most dangerous area; only trained and protected members enter it."},{question:"Why is a person who was near a chemical release decontaminated before going to hospital?",options:[{text:"So that the hospital does not need to examine the person.",correct:!1},{text:"So that the person can return home at once.",correct:!1},{text:"Because decontamination replaces the medical treatment the person would get in hospital.",correct:!1},{text:"Because the material on clothes and skin can harm the person and others.",correct:!0}],explanation:"Decontamination reduces harm to the person and prevents spreading the material to family and staff; medical care is still needed."},{question:"A worker finds an unknown powder leaking from a package. What is the right first action?",options:[{text:"Smell it carefully to identify it.",correct:!1},{text:"Leave it untouched, move people away and report it.",correct:!0},{text:"Sweep it carefully into a plastic bag and throw it in the waste bin.",correct:!1},{text:"Carry the package outside to the street.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Unknown materials are never touched, smelled or moved; distance and a report protect everyone."},{question:"In a radiation emergency, what is the public advice?",options:[{text:"Get in a car and drive toward the site.",correct:!1},{text:"Stay outside where the air is open.",correct:!1},{text:"Get inside, stay inside and stay tuned.",correct:!0},{text:"Wait for neighbors to decide.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Being inside a building reduces exposure; official information tells people what to do next."}]},{id:9,code:"OPS-DMC-109",icon:"ClipboardList",accent:"#dc2626",title:"The disaster management cycle",subtitle:"Prevention, preparedness, response and recovery",summary:"The work of Civil Defense does not start when the call comes and does not end when the fire is out. In this lesson you learn the four phases of the disaster management cycle and how each phase reduces the harm of the next disaster.",blocks:[{heading:"Why a cycle?",body:"A disaster is not a single moment. There is work before it, during it and after it, and each part affects the others. Disaster management is usually divided into four phases: prevention and mitigation, preparedness, response and recovery. They are drawn as a circle because the lessons learned during recovery return to prevention: a community that understands why the last flood harmed it can reduce the harm of the next one. The response phase is the one people see on television, but most lives are saved by work done long before, such as a building rule, a cleaned drainage channel or a family that has practiced its plan. Civil Defense works in all four phases."},{heading:"Hazard, risk and vulnerability",body:"A hazard is an event or condition that may cause harm, such as a flood, an earthquake, a fire or a chemical release. A hazard becomes a disaster when it seriously disrupts a community and exceeds its ability to cope with its own resources. Disaster risk depends on the hazard, on who and what is exposed to it, on how vulnerable they are and on their capacity to cope. For example, the same heavy rain may cause no harm in one neighborhood and a disaster in another, where houses stand in a drainage channel, the drains are blocked and no one knows where to go. Reducing exposure and vulnerability and building capacity is the heart of disaster management."},{heading:"Prevention and mitigation",body:"Prevention means avoiding new risk and removing existing risk where possible: not building in flood channels, following safety requirements in buildings, storing fuel and chemicals correctly and inspecting public places. Mitigation means reducing the harm when a hazard cannot be prevented: an earthquake cannot be stopped, but buildings that follow construction rules and furniture fixed to walls reduce the injuries. Awareness is part of prevention: a public that knows the dangers avoids many accidents. Lesson 10 explains how Civil Defense carries out prevention through inspection, safety requirements and public awareness."},{heading:"Preparedness",body:"Preparedness is everything done before a disaster so that the response is fast and organized: emergency plans, training and drills, warning systems, stores of equipment and supplies, agreements between agencies, and family emergency plans and kits. Civil Defense keeps its teams, vehicles and equipment ready, trains its members and takes part in exercises with other bodies. Institutions such as schools, factories and hospitals prepare their own plans and drills. A family is prepared when every member knows the emergency numbers 115, 122 and 104, the meeting place and the way out of the house."},{heading:"Response and recovery",body:"Response is the action taken during and directly after a disaster to save lives, reduce harm and meet people's basic needs: warning, rescue, firefighting, first aid, evacuation and shelter, as described in lessons 3 to 7. Recovery is the longer work of restoring services, housing, livelihoods and health, including psychological support. A good recovery does not rebuild the same risk: the idea of building back better means rebuilding in a safer way and in safer places. At the international level, the Hyogo Framework for Action (2005-2015) and the Sendai Framework for Disaster Risk Reduction (2015-2030) guide countries; Sendai has four priorities: understanding disaster risk, strengthening disaster risk governance, investing in risk reduction, and enhancing preparedness for effective response and to build back better in recovery."}],steps:[{title:"Identify the hazards",detail:"List the hazards that can affect your area or workplace: flood, earthquake, fire, chemical release or severe weather."},{title:"Assess the risk",detail:"Ask who and what is exposed, who is most vulnerable (children, elderly people, people with disabilities) and what capacity exists."},{title:"Reduce the risk",detail:"Remove or reduce the risk where possible: maintenance, safety requirements, correct storage and awareness."},{title:"Prepare",detail:"Write a plan, train people, run drills and keep equipment and supplies ready."},{title:"Respond according to the plan",detail:"When the disaster comes, life comes first: warn, evacuate or shelter, call 115 and follow the incident commander.",critical:!0},{title:"Recover and learn",detail:"Restore services in a safer way and review what went well and what must change, then return to prevention."}],warnings:["Do not wait for a disaster to start planning: in the first minutes there is no time to decide who does what.","Do not rebuild in the same dangerous place in the same way without asking the competent authority: the same disaster may return.","Do not treat a drill as a formality: a drill that is not taken seriously gives false confidence."],tips:["Learn the hazards of your own area; they are the starting point of every plan.","After every incident or drill, write down three things that went well and three that must improve.","Include people with disabilities and elderly people when you plan, not after.","Keep your family emergency plan short and practice it once in a while."],activity:{prompt:"A neighborhood has been flooded twice in recent years. The houses were repaired each time but nothing else changed. Which action belongs to prevention and mitigation?",options:[{text:"Sending more rescue boats the next time it floods.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: rescue boats are part of the response; they do not reduce the risk."},{text:"Cleaning and widening the drainage channels and stopping new building in the flood path, as the competent authority directs.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: this reduces the hazard and the exposure before the next flood."},{text:"Collecting donations after the next flood.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: donations are part of recovery and come after the harm."},{text:"Repairing the houses again in the same way.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: this rebuilds the same risk instead of reducing it."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"Heavy rain has flooded a district and water is rising in the streets. Civil Defense teams are on the scene. Which phase is this, and what comes first?",options:[{text:"Response: warning, evacuating and rescuing the people in danger.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: during the disaster, saving lives is the first task of the response phase."},{text:"Recovery: assessing the damage to houses.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: recovery starts when no one is still in danger."},{text:"Prevention: planning new drainage channels.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: prevention work is needed, but not while people are in danger."}]},{prompt:"A month later the water has gone. Some residents want to rebuild quickly exactly where the water entered. What does the cycle advise?",options:[{text:"Rebuild quickly in the same way so that life returns to normal.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: rebuilding the same risk means the same disaster may return."},{text:"Leave the district without any plan.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: recovery needs a plan, not abandonment."},{text:"Rebuild according to the competent authority's plan in a safer way and review the lessons learned for the next flood.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: building back better and learning lessons closes the cycle and returns to prevention."},{text:"Wait for the next flood to see whether it is really dangerous.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: the last flood already showed the risk."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: every winter, water collected in the lower streets of a small town. One year a heavy storm flooded several houses and Civil Defense teams evacuated families by night. During recovery the residents and the local authority reviewed what had happened. They found blocked drains and houses built in the old stream bed. The drains were cleaned before each winter, a warning plan was agreed with Civil Defense and families practiced where to go. The next storm was as strong, but no one had to be rescued at night.",quiz:[{question:"A school writes an evacuation plan and runs a drill. Which phase of the cycle is this?",options:[{text:"Response.",correct:!1},{text:"Recovery.",correct:!1},{text:"Preparedness.",correct:!0},{text:"Mitigation after the event.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Plans and drills done before an emergency are preparedness."},{question:"Why is disaster management drawn as a circle?",options:[{text:"Because lessons learned in recovery return to prevention.",correct:!0},{text:"Because disasters always come at the same time each year.",correct:!1},{text:"Because the four phases have the same length.",correct:!1},{text:"Because response is always the first phase.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The circle shows that each disaster teaches lessons that reduce the risk of the next one."},{question:"The same heavy rain causes a disaster in one neighborhood and no harm in another. What explains the difference?",options:[{text:"Only bad luck.",correct:!1},{text:"The rain in that neighborhood was stronger than the forecast said.",correct:!1},{text:"Rain always causes a disaster.",correct:!1},{text:"Different exposure, vulnerability and capacity.",correct:!0}],explanation:"Risk depends on the hazard and also on exposure, vulnerability and capacity, which people can change."},{question:"An earthquake cannot be prevented. Which action is mitigation?",options:[{text:"Waiting for the earthquake to end.",correct:!1},{text:"Fixing heavy furniture to the walls and following construction rules.",correct:!0},{text:"Rebuilding all the damaged houses after the earthquake in exactly the same way.",correct:!1},{text:"Calling 115 during the shaking.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Mitigation reduces the harm of a hazard that cannot be stopped."},{question:"Which of these is part of recovery?",options:[{text:"Rescuing people trapped under a collapsed building in the first hours.",correct:!1},{text:"Training a rescue team and checking its boats before the season of floods.",correct:!1},{text:"Restoring services and helping families rebuild more safely.",correct:!0},{text:"Warning people that a storm is coming.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Recovery restores services, housing and livelihoods after the immediate danger."},{question:"What does build back better mean?",options:[{text:"Rebuilding more safely and in safer places.",correct:!0},{text:"Rebuilding as fast as possible with exactly the same design and in the same place as before.",correct:!1},{text:"Building larger houses than before the disaster.",correct:!1},{text:"Leaving damaged areas empty forever.",correct:!1}],explanation:"It means using recovery to reduce the risk, not to rebuild it."}]},{id:10,code:"OPS-PRV-110",icon:"ClipboardCheck",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Prevention: inspection, safety requirements and public awareness",subtitle:"Stopping the emergency before it starts",summary:"Most fires and many accidents can be prevented. In this lesson you learn how Civil Defense prevents emergencies through inspection, safety requirements and public awareness, and what owners, managers and citizens are responsible for.",blocks:[{heading:"Why prevention comes first",body:"A fire that never starts needs no fire truck, and an exit that is open on the day of a fire saves more lives than any rescue. That is why prevention is one of the principles of civil defense work. Prevention has three main tools: inspection of buildings and workplaces, safety requirements that owners must meet, and public awareness that teaches people to avoid danger. In the Duhok Directorate this work is linked to the department of prevention, inspection and safety and the department of media and awareness. Prevention is quiet work, and its success is measured by the emergencies that do not happen."},{heading:"Safety requirements",body:`Safety requirements are the conditions a building or workplace must meet so that a fire or emergency does not start easily and people can get out safely if it does. In general they include:

• Exits that are clear, marked and never locked against people leaving.
• Emergency lighting and exit signs where they are required.
• Fire detection and alarm suitable for the building.
• Suitable fire extinguishers that are inspected and maintained.
• Correct storage of fuel, gas cylinders and chemicals, away from exits and heat.
• Safe electrical installations without overloaded sockets.
• An evacuation plan, drills and a person responsible for safety.

The exact requirements, permits and certificates for each type of place are set by law and by the competent authority; this lesson does not state them.`},{heading:"How an inspection is carried out",body:"An inspection is a visit by an authorized inspector to check whether a place meets the safety requirements. The inspector prepares by reading the previous findings, then walks through the building with the person responsible. Life-safety items are checked first: exits and corridors, alarms, extinguishers and emergency lighting. Then storage, electrical installations, cleanliness and the records of maintenance and drills are checked. Every finding is recorded and explained to the owner, with the correction required. A follow-up visit checks that the deficiencies were corrected. A serious danger to life, such as a locked exit during an event, is reported at once to the competent authority. The inspector is impartial and courteous and does not accept gifts."},{heading:"Public awareness",body:"Awareness means giving people the knowledge to avoid danger and to act correctly when it comes. Civil Defense carries out awareness through campaigns, visits to schools and workplaces, lectures, media messages and open days. Good awareness messages are short, simple, consistent and taken from authorized sources; they target the people most at risk, such as children, elderly people and families in old houses. Awareness is planned for the season: heating fires in winter, drowning and heat in summer. This app is part of that work. The effect is measured by changes in behavior, not by the number of leaflets."},{heading:"The role of owners, managers and citizens",body:"The owner or manager of a building is responsible for its safety every day, not only on the day of an inspection. Inspection is a partnership, not a punishment: the inspector points out the danger and the owner corrects it. Correct the deficiencies in the time the competent authority sets, keep maintenance records and train your staff. Citizens help prevention too: they do not block exits, do not store fuel in stairwells and report serious hazards they see. If a hazard threatens life now, call 115. Follow the instructions of Civil Defense and the competent authority in all matters of inspection and permits."}],steps:[{title:"Prepare",detail:"Read the previous findings and the type of the place, and agree the visit with the person responsible."},{title:"Walk through with the person responsible",detail:"Visit every floor and area, including stores, roofs and plant rooms, together with the person responsible."},{title:"Check life safety first",detail:"Exits and corridors, alarms, extinguishers and emergency lighting come before everything else.",critical:!0},{title:"Record and explain",detail:"Write down every finding and explain to the owner what must be corrected and why."},{title:"Act on serious danger at once",detail:"A locked exit or another danger to life is reported to the competent authority immediately and corrected before people use the place.",critical:!0},{title:"Follow up and raise awareness",detail:"Check that deficiencies were corrected and leave the staff with simple safety messages."}],warnings:["Never lock or block an emergency exit, even to prevent theft: in a fire a locked exit can trap and kill people.","Do not hide deficiencies or move equipment in only for the inspection: the danger stays when the inspector leaves.","Do not offer gifts or favors to an inspector: it is wrong and it puts lives at risk."],tips:["Walk your exits every morning: open, clear and marked.","Keep a simple record of extinguisher checks, maintenance and drills.","Ask Civil Defense for official awareness materials instead of copying messages from social media.","Plan awareness by season: heating in winter, water and heat in summer."],activity:{prompt:"A shop owner keeps gas cylinders stacked beside the only exit because there is no other space. An inspector points it out. What is the best response from the owner?",options:[{text:"Hide the cylinders in the back room until the inspector leaves.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: the danger stays; hiding it only moves it out of sight."},{text:"Argue that nothing has happened in years.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: the absence of past accidents does not make a hazard safe."},{text:"Wait for a written penalty before doing anything.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: the aim is safety, not avoiding a penalty; the danger exists now."},{text:"Move the cylinders to a safe ventilated place away from the exit and ask how to store them correctly.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: correcting the deficiency and learning the right method is the purpose of inspection."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"During an inspection of an events hall, the inspector finds the rear exit chained and locked. What does the inspector do first?",options:[{text:"Note it for the next annual visit.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: a locked exit is a danger to life that cannot wait."},{text:"Unlock it personally and leave without recording it.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: the finding must be recorded and the cause corrected."},{text:"Explain the danger, record it and report it to the competent authority so that the exit is opened before the hall is used.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: life-safety items come first and serious danger is reported at once."}]},{prompt:"The owner says a wedding is booked for tonight and the key holder is away. What is the right outcome?",options:[{text:"The event goes ahead as planned with the exit locked.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: hundreds of guests could be trapped in a fire."},{text:"The exit is opened and kept free of locks and obstacles before any guest enters, as the competent authority instructs.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: no event is worth a locked exit; the authority's instructions decide the next steps."},{text:"The guests are told to use the main door only.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: in a fire the main door may be blocked by smoke or flames."},{text:"The inspector accepts an invitation to the wedding.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: accepting favors damages impartiality."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: during a routine visit to a small factory, an inspector found extinguishers that had not been checked for a long time, cardboard stored in the stairwell and an alarm that did not sound. She explained each finding to the manager and recorded them. The manager cleared the stairwell that day, arranged maintenance and trained the staff. Some time later a small fire started in a machine. The alarm sounded, the workers left by the clear stairwell and a trained worker reported it to 115. No one was hurt.",quiz:[{question:"Which of these is the best example of prevention?",options:[{text:"Rescuing people trapped in a lift.",correct:!1},{text:"Inspecting a building and correcting a blocked exit.",correct:!0},{text:"Putting out a small fire that started in a restaurant kitchen.",correct:!1},{text:"Treating a burn after an accident.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Prevention removes the danger before an emergency; the others are response."},{question:"A manager locks the emergency exit at night shifts to stop theft. What is the problem?",options:[{text:"There is no problem as long as the key is kept in the manager's office.",correct:!1},{text:"It is only a problem during the day.",correct:!1},{text:"The exit only matters during inspections.",correct:!1},{text:"People could be trapped if a fire starts at night.",correct:!0}],explanation:"Exits must never be locked against people leaving while anyone is inside."},{question:"During an inspection, what is checked first?",options:[{text:"Exits, alarms and extinguishers.",correct:!0},{text:"The color of the walls and the furniture.",correct:!1},{text:"The number of customers per day.",correct:!1},{text:"The cleanliness and tidiness of the manager's office and the reception area.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Life-safety items come first because they decide whether people can get out."},{question:"Who is responsible for the safety of a building every day?",options:[{text:"Only the Civil Defense inspector.",correct:!1},{text:"The customers who visit the building.",correct:!1},{text:"The owner or manager of the building.",correct:!0},{text:"Nobody until there is an accident.",correct:!1}],explanation:"The inspector checks, but the owner or manager is responsible for safety every day."},{question:"Which awareness message fits winter best?",options:[{text:"How to avoid drowning in rivers.",correct:!1},{text:"How to use heaters safely and ventilate rooms.",correct:!0},{text:"How to protect yourself from extreme heat.",correct:!1},{text:"How to behave safely at a family picnic on a hot summer day.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Awareness is planned by season; heating fires and fumes are winter risks."},{question:"Where should the exact inspection rules and permits for a place come from?",options:[{text:"From a post on social media.",correct:!1},{text:"From a neighbor who owns a shop.",correct:!1},{text:"From the inspector's personal opinion.",correct:!1},{text:"From the law and the competent authority.",correct:!0}],explanation:"Requirements, permits and deadlines are set by law and the competent authority."}]},{id:11,code:"OPS-VOL-111",icon:"HeartHandshake",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Volunteers and community participation",subtitle:"Helping safely, as part of an organized effort",summary:"In the first minutes of an emergency, neighbors are often the first people on the scene. In this lesson you learn how volunteers and the community can support Civil Defense safely, what volunteers should and should not do, and how a community prepares.",blocks:[{heading:"Why the community matters",body:"Civil Defense cannot be everywhere at once. In a large disaster many incidents happen at the same time, roads may be blocked and teams may need time to arrive. In those minutes the people nearby are the first to help: they call 115, warn others, guide the team, care for the injured within their knowledge and look after children and elderly people. A community that is prepared multiplies the capacity of Civil Defense. Help is most useful when it is organized and safe; well-meaning but disorganized help can block roads, put helpers in danger and create a second emergency."},{heading:"What volunteers can do",body:`Trained volunteers can support Civil Defense in many ways that do not require entering danger:

• Awareness in schools, mosques, workplaces and neighborhoods.
• First aid within their training.
• Helping to evacuate people who need assistance.
• Supporting shelters: registering families, distributing water, food and blankets.
• Caring for children, elderly people and people with disabilities.
• Logistics: carrying supplies, guiding vehicles, keeping roads clear.
• Basic psychological support: listening, calming, and connecting people with services.

Entering burning or collapsed buildings, water rescue, hazardous materials and suspected explosive objects are the work of trained specialists with equipment. A volunteer does not do these tasks.`},{heading:"Rules for a volunteer at an incident",body:`A volunteer is part of a team, not a hero working alone. These rules keep volunteers and the people they help safe:

• Register and report to the person in charge before doing anything; do not go to an incident on your own.
• Take only the task you are given and stay within your training.
• Your own safety comes first: an injured volunteer becomes another casualty.
• Work in pairs or teams and tell your team leader where you are.
• Wear the identification you are given and never pretend to be a Civil Defense member.
• Respect the dignity and privacy of affected people: no photos of victims and no sharing of their information.
• Pass on only official information; do not spread rumors.`},{heading:"How a community prepares",body:"A community can prepare before an emergency in simple ways. Neighbors can agree on meeting points, a way of contacting one another and who checks on elderly people or families with young children, with their consent. It helps to know which residents have useful skills, such as nurses, drivers or engineers, and which buildings can serve as meeting places. Schools and community centers can host awareness sessions and drills with Civil Defense. All of this is done in coordination with Civil Defense and the competent authority, so that community efforts fit the official plan and do not compete with it."},{heading:"Joining and looking after yourself",body:"Anyone who wants to volunteer should ask the Civil Defense Directorate through its official channels whether volunteer registration and training are available and what the conditions are. This lesson does not state those conditions because they are set by the Directorate and the competent authority. Training comes before field work: a volunteer who has practiced is safer and more useful. Volunteering can be hard: helpers see suffering and work long hours. Rest, eat, drink water and talk with your team or someone you trust about what you saw. Asking for support is part of doing the work well."}],steps:[{title:"Register and report",detail:"Report to the person in charge and register before you do anything; never go into an incident area on your own.",critical:!0},{title:"Receive your task",detail:"Listen to the briefing: your task, your team leader, the dangers and how to communicate."},{title:"Check your safety",detail:"Make sure you have the equipment and clothing for the task and that the area you are sent to is safe.",critical:!0},{title:"Work in a team within your training",detail:"Work in pairs or teams, do only what you are trained for and stop if the situation becomes dangerous.",critical:!0},{title:"Report back and hand over",detail:"Tell your team leader what you did and what you saw, and hand over any person or task you leave."},{title:"Rest and talk",detail:"Rest, eat and drink, and talk about the experience with your team at the end of the shift."}],warnings:["Do not go into a fire, a collapsed building, floodwater or a hazardous area to help: wait for the trained team and give them information.","Do not photograph victims or share their names, injuries or location: it harms their dignity and privacy.","Do not wear a uniform or sign you were not given or present yourself as Civil Defense staff."],tips:["Take a first aid course before you need it.","Keep a list of the elderly people and families with small children on your street, with their consent.","Agree with your neighbors on a meeting point and who checks on whom.","Ask the Directorate through official channels about volunteering instead of turning up at an incident."],activity:{prompt:"After an earthquake, voices are heard from a partly collapsed house. Several neighbors want to climb in and dig. As a trained volunteer, what do you do?",options:[{text:"Keep people off the unstable building, call 115 with the exact location of the voices and help with tasks the team gives you.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: entering an unstable building is specialist work; accurate information and order save the victims and the helpers."},{text:"Lead the neighbors inside because time matters.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: an aftershock or further collapse could trap the helpers too."},{text:"Start pulling out the large pieces of concrete by hand.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: moving debris without training can bring the rest of the structure down on the victims."},{text:"Leave the scene because volunteers cannot help.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: volunteers help by keeping order, giving information and supporting families."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"Families who left their homes in a flood are arriving at a shelter. You want to help. What do you do first?",options:[{text:"Start distributing food from your car to anyone you see.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: uncoordinated distribution causes crowding and leaves some families out."},{text:"Report to the shelter manager, register and take the task you are given.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: help is most useful when it is organized under the person in charge."},{text:"Post on social media asking everyone to come to the shelter.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: crowds of people without a task block the shelter and the road."}]},{prompt:"You are registering families. A relative asks you to photograph a sick elderly man and post it online to ask for help. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Take the photo and post it, because it may bring help.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: it harms the man's dignity and privacy, and help comes faster through the shelter's services."},{text:"Ignore the request and continue registering.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: the man may need medical care; his condition must be passed on."},{text:"Explain that photos are not shared and tell the shelter manager or the medical team about the man at once.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: protecting dignity and getting him care are both part of the volunteer's role."},{text:"Give the family your own medicines for him.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: medicines are given only by the medical team."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: before winter, a group of neighbors attended an awareness session with Civil Defense. They agreed on a meeting point, made a list of elderly residents with their consent and noted who could drive. When heavy rain flooded the lower street one night, they called 115, knocked on the doors of the elderly residents and gathered everyone at the meeting point. When the team arrived, the neighbors gave them the list and the location of one family that had not answered. The team reached that family quickly.",quiz:[{question:"A volunteer arrives at an incident. What is the first thing to do?",options:[{text:"Start helping wherever help is needed.",correct:!1},{text:"Take photos to document the damage.",correct:!1},{text:"Report to the person in charge and register.",correct:!0},{text:"Find the most dangerous place and go there.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Organized help starts with registering and taking a task from the person in charge."},{question:"Which task is suitable for a trained volunteer?",options:[{text:"Registering families and distributing water at a shelter.",correct:!0},{text:"Entering a burning house to look for people.",correct:!1},{text:"Moving a suspected explosive object away from a road.",correct:!1},{text:"Swimming into floodwater to reach a car.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Volunteers support in safe roles; the others are specialist work with equipment."},{question:"Why must a volunteer stay within their training?",options:[{text:"Because training certificates are needed for promotion.",correct:!1},{text:"Because the team leader dislikes questions.",correct:!1},{text:"Because volunteers are never allowed to help.",correct:!1},{text:"Because acting beyond training can injure the volunteer and the victim.",correct:!0}],explanation:"Untrained actions can make injuries worse and create new casualties."},{question:"A volunteer is asked by a journalist for the names of injured people. What is the right answer?",options:[{text:"Give the names because the public has a right to know.",correct:!1},{text:"Refer the journalist to the official spokesperson.",correct:!0},{text:"Give only the names of the seriously injured.",correct:!1},{text:"Share a photo instead of the names.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Affected people's information is private; official information comes from the authorized spokesperson."},{question:"How can a community prepare before an emergency?",options:[{text:"Agree on meeting points and who checks on elderly neighbors.",correct:!0},{text:"Wait for Civil Defense to do all the planning.",correct:!1},{text:"Store extra fuel in the stairwell for emergencies.",correct:!1},{text:"Keep the plan secret from new neighbors.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Simple shared arrangements make the first minutes faster and safer."},{question:"After several long shifts a volunteer cannot sleep and keeps thinking about what they saw. What is the right step?",options:[{text:"Hide it and work even longer.",correct:!1},{text:"Stop volunteering forever without telling anyone.",correct:!1},{text:"Rest and talk with someone they trust.",correct:!0},{text:"Watch videos of the incident again.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Helpers also need care; rest and talking about it are part of doing the work well."}]},{id:12,code:"OPS-COO-112",icon:"Network",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Working with other agencies and the incident command system",subtitle:"One incident, one plan, many agencies",summary:"Large emergencies need more than one agency. In this lesson you learn how Civil Defense works with the ambulance service, the police and other bodies, and how the incident command system keeps everyone working to one plan.",blocks:[{heading:"No agency works alone",body:"At a serious road crash with a fire, Civil Defense puts out the fire and frees the trapped people, the ambulance service (122) treats and transports the injured, and the police (104) control traffic, secure the scene and investigate. At a flood or an earthquake many more bodies join: health services and hospitals, the municipality with heavy machinery and water and road services, electricity and gas services that cut the supply, relief organizations, and the army or other forces when the competent authority requests them. Each agency has its own skills, equipment and legal responsibilities. The incident is handled well only when they work together, share information and do not get in each other's way."},{heading:"The incident command system",body:`The incident command system (ICS) is an international model for organizing the response to any incident, small or large. Its main principles are:

• One incident commander is responsible for the whole incident.
• A clear chain of command: every person reports to one supervisor.
• Common terms and plain language instead of agency codes.
• A manageable span of control: no supervisor leads more people than they can direct safely.
• A modular organization that grows or shrinks with the incident.
• An incident action plan with clear objectives.
• Known facilities: a command post and a staging area for arriving resources.
• Accountability: everyone checks in and is tracked until they leave.`},{heading:"The structure of command",body:"In a small incident the incident commander does everything. As it grows, the commander delegates. The command staff support the commander: a safety officer watches for dangers to responders, a public information officer gives official information to the media and the public, and a liaison officer is the contact for other agencies. The general staff lead four sections: operations does the work on the ground, planning collects information and prepares the plan, logistics provides equipment, food, fuel and transport, and finance and administration track costs and records. Only the parts that are needed are set up. When a more senior officer arrives, command is transferred with a briefing, never silently."},{heading:"Unified command and the situation report",body:"When several agencies share responsibility, they form a unified command: their leaders work together at one command post and agree one set of objectives and one plan, while each agency keeps its own authority over its staff. Joint working rests on simple principles: be at the same place, communicate clearly, coordinate tasks, understand the risks together and share the same picture of the incident. A structured situation report helps every agency understand the same thing. One common format is METHANE: major incident declared or not, exact location, type of incident, hazards, access routes, number and type of casualties, and emergency services present and needed."},{heading:"The public and official information",body:"For the public, good coordination means a few simple rules. Call the right number: 115 for fire and rescue, 122 for the ambulance, 104 for the police; any of them will pass the call on if needed. Do not cross a cordon or tape: it protects you and keeps routes open for the teams. Follow the instructions of the police and Civil Defense at the scene. Take information only from the official spokesperson and official channels, because rumors cause panic and block the work. This lesson does not state which agency leads which type of incident in Duhok; that is set by law and by the competent authority."}],steps:[{title:"Take command and size up",detail:"The first officer to arrive takes command, checks the dangers and decides what resources are needed.",critical:!0},{title:"Send a situation report",detail:"Report in a fixed order such as METHANE so that every agency understands the same picture."},{title:"Set up the scene",detail:"Choose a command post and a staging area, and set the cordon with the police.",critical:!0},{title:"Form a unified command",detail:"Agency leaders meet at the command post and agree one set of objectives and one plan."},{title:"Assign tasks and account for people",detail:"Give each team a clear task and a supervisor, and track everyone who enters and leaves.",critical:!0},{title:"Hand over and review",detail:"Transfer command with a briefing, close the incident in order and review it with all agencies."}],warnings:["Do not freelance: a team that acts outside the plan can endanger itself and others and waste resources.","Do not use agency codes on shared radio channels: use plain language that every agency understands.","Do not cross a cordon to watch or film: you put yourself at risk and block the routes of the teams."],tips:["Learn the names of the ICS roles; they are the same whatever the size of the incident.","Practice METHANE reports in drills until they come naturally.","In joint exercises, meet the people from other agencies before the real emergency.","Write down the time of every decision and handover."],activity:{prompt:"A truck has crashed into a bus. There is a fire in the engine and some passengers are trapped. Civil Defense, the ambulance service and the police arrive. Which division of work is right?",options:[{text:"Every agency does everything and the first to arrive gives orders to the others' staff.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: without one plan the agencies duplicate work and get in each other's way."},{text:"The police treat the injured while Civil Defense directs traffic.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: each agency works within its own skills and responsibilities."},{text:"Civil Defense fights the fire and frees the trapped, the ambulance treats and transports, the police control traffic and the scene, all under one coordinated command.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: each agency does its part and one command keeps them working to the same plan."},{text:"They wait for a senior official before anyone starts work.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: the first officer takes command at once; command is transferred when a senior officer arrives."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"At a building collapse, officers from three agencies are each giving different orders to the same volunteers. What should happen?",options:[{text:"The agency leaders form a unified command at one command post and agree one plan and one chain of orders.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: unified command removes conflicting orders while each agency keeps its authority."},{text:"The agency with the most staff takes over and the others leave.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: every agency's skills are needed; leaving loses capacity."},{text:"The volunteers choose which officer to follow.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: this keeps the confusion and endangers the volunteers."}]},{prompt:"Reporters are asking any responder they meet how many people have died. Different numbers are spreading. What is the right step?",options:[{text:"Each agency gives its own numbers to the media.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: different numbers create confusion and rumors."},{text:"A single public information officer gives verified information for all agencies at set times.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: one official voice gives the public consistent and verified information."},{text:"Responders stop the reporters from working.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: the media help inform the public; they need an official source."},{text:"Nothing is said until the incident is over.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: the public needs safety information during the incident."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: heavy rain caused a landslide that blocked a road and damaged two houses. The first Civil Defense officer took command and sent a METHANE report. The police closed the road and set the cordon, the ambulance service set up near the staging area and the municipality brought machinery. The leaders met at one command post and agreed the plan: rescue first, then clearing the road. A single officer briefed the media every hour. Two residents were rescued, no responder was hurt and the road reopened in order.",quiz:[{question:"What is the main purpose of the incident command system?",options:[{text:"To decide which agency gets the credit for the rescue afterward.",correct:!1},{text:"To organize all responders under one plan and a clear chain of command.",correct:!0},{text:"To replace the work of the ambulance service.",correct:!1},{text:"To reduce the number of agencies allowed to work at the scene.",correct:!1}],explanation:"ICS gives every incident one plan, one commander and clear lines of reporting."},{question:"Several agencies share responsibility for an incident. What is formed?",options:[{text:"Separate command posts for each agency.",correct:!1},{text:"A committee that meets after the incident.",correct:!1},{text:"A chain where the police lead every incident.",correct:!1},{text:"A unified command with one plan.",correct:!0}],explanation:"In unified command, agency leaders agree one plan while each keeps its authority."},{question:"Which ICS section provides equipment, food, fuel and transport?",options:[{text:"Logistics.",correct:!0},{text:"Operations.",correct:!1},{text:"Planning.",correct:!1},{text:"Finance and administration.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Logistics supports the response with resources; operations does the work on the ground."},{question:"Why should plain language be used on shared radio channels?",options:[{text:"Because using codes on any radio is forbidden by law.",correct:!1},{text:"Because radios cannot transmit codes.",correct:!1},{text:"Because every agency must understand the message the same way.",correct:!0},{text:"Because plain language is always shorter and faster than codes.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Different agencies use different codes; plain language prevents dangerous misunderstandings."},{question:"A senior officer arrives at an incident already under way. How is command transferred?",options:[{text:"Silently, as soon as the senior officer arrives.",correct:!1},{text:"Only after the incident ends.",correct:!1},{text:"By radio without meeting the current commander.",correct:!1},{text:"With a face-to-face briefing on the situation and the plan.",correct:!0}],explanation:"A briefing makes sure the new commander knows the situation, the resources and the plan."},{question:"A citizen sees a house fire and an injured person. Which call is right?",options:[{text:"104 only, because the police lead every incident.",correct:!1},{text:"115, and say there is an injured person.",correct:!0},{text:"122 only, and leave the fire.",correct:!1},{text:"Wait for someone else to call.",correct:!1}],explanation:"115 handles fire and rescue and will coordinate with the ambulance service; tell them about the injured person."}]},{id:13,code:"OPS-EQP-113",icon:"Truck",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Vehicles, equipment and the emergency operations room",subtitle:"The tools of the work and the room that directs them",summary:"Every response depends on vehicles and equipment that are ready and on an operations room that sends the right team to the right place. In this lesson you learn the general types of Civil Defense vehicles and equipment, how the operations room works and how the public can help.",blocks:[{heading:"Readiness is part of the response",body:"A team is only as fast as its vehicle and only as safe as its equipment. A pump that does not start or a breathing set with an empty cylinder can cost lives. That is why vehicles and equipment are checked at the start of every shift, cleaned and refilled after every incident and maintained on a schedule, with records of each check. In the Duhok Directorate, the logistics and equipment department supports the readiness of vehicles and equipment and organizes supplies and maintenance. This lesson describes general types used by civil defense services; it does not list the vehicles or equipment of any particular directorate."},{heading:"Types of vehicles",body:`Civil defense services use different vehicles for different tasks. General types include:

• Fire engines that carry water, a pump, hoses and firefighting equipment.
• Water tankers that bring more water where supply is limited.
• Aerial ladders and platforms for rescue and firefighting at height.
• Rescue vehicles with cutting and lifting tools, lighting and generators.
• Hazardous materials units with detection and protective equipment.
• Water rescue units with boats and water rescue equipment.
• Command and communication vehicles for large incidents.
• Support and supply vehicles for fuel, food and equipment.

Which vehicles a directorate has, and how many, depends on its needs and resources.`},{heading:"Equipment and personal protection",body:'Firefighters and rescuers use personal protective equipment: fire-resistant clothing, a helmet, gloves and boots, and breathing apparatus where there is smoke or toxic gas. Firefighting equipment includes hoses, nozzles, extinguishers and ladders. Rescue equipment includes hydraulic cutting and spreading tools, lifting equipment, ropes and stretchers. Teams also use first aid kits, thermal imaging cameras to find people and hot spots in smoke, gas detectors, radios and lighting. Every item has two rules: only trained members use it, and it is checked before and after use. Equipment that is damaged or overdue for maintenance is taken out of service, not used "just this once".'},{heading:"The emergency operations room",body:"The emergency operations room is the nerve center of the response. It receives 115 calls and reports, records them and sends suitable teams. The call taker asks first for the location, then what happened, how many people are hurt or trapped and what dangers there are, and keeps the caller on the line when needed. The room tracks where every unit is, sends more resources when the incident grows, coordinates with the ambulance service (122), the police (104) and other bodies, and informs the leadership. It keeps a log of times and decisions. Because emergencies can happen at any hour, the room works day and night."},{heading:"How the public helps",body:`The public can make the work of vehicles and the operations room faster:

• When you hear a siren, move aside safely and let the vehicle pass; do not follow it.
• Do not park in front of fire hydrants, building entrances or narrow lanes that a fire engine needs.
• Never make false calls: they occupy the line and can send a team away from a real emergency.
• Keep your house number or a clear landmark visible, and give exact directions on the phone.
• When the team arrives, meet them, give them information and then keep back.`}],steps:[{title:"Receive the call",detail:"The operations room takes the location first, then what happened, how many people and the dangers.",critical:!0},{title:"Dispatch suitable units",detail:"Send the vehicles and teams the incident needs and tell the crews what to expect."},{title:"Drive safely",detail:"Use lights and sirens as allowed and drive with care: a vehicle that crashes helps no one.",critical:!0},{title:"Position at the scene",detail:"Park so the vehicle is safe from the danger, does not block other units and can work and leave.",critical:!0},{title:"Track and support",detail:"The operations room follows the incident, sends more resources and coordinates with other agencies."},{title:"Return and restore readiness",detail:"Clean, refill and check the vehicle and equipment, record any fault and report ready."}],warnings:["Do not block or follow an emergency vehicle: every minute of delay can cost a life, and following it puts you in danger.","Never make a false call to 115: it can send a team away from a real emergency.","Do not use rescue or firefighting equipment you are not trained on, even if it is left near you."],tips:["Save 115, 122 and 104 in your phone and teach them to your family.","Know the nearest landmark to your home so you can guide the team quickly.","Leave fire hydrants and building access clear when you park.","If you work with equipment, record every check and report faults at once."],activity:{prompt:"You are driving on a busy road and hear a siren behind you. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Brake hard and stop in the middle of the lane.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: sudden stopping in the lane can cause a crash and still blocks the vehicle."},{text:"Signal, move aside safely when you can and let the vehicle pass, then do not follow it.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: making a safe path quickly helps the team arrive without causing another accident."},{text:"Speed up to stay ahead of the emergency vehicle.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: speeding is dangerous and does not clear the way."},{text:"Follow closely behind it to get through the traffic faster.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: following an emergency vehicle is dangerous and may block the scene."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:'A caller to 115 is shouting: "Fire, come quickly!" What does the call taker ask first?',options:[{text:"The exact location of the fire.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: without the location no team can be sent, even if the line is cut."},{text:"The caller's full name and age.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: the name is useful, but the location comes first."},{text:"What caused the fire.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: the cause is investigated later; it does not decide the response."}]},{prompt:"At the same time, a road crash with trapped people and a small rubbish fire are reported, and only a few units are free. What does the operations room do?",options:[{text:"Send units to the call that came first.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: the order of the calls does not decide priority; risk to life does."},{text:"Send all units to the rubbish fire because it is closer.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: distance alone does not decide; trapped people are the priority."},{text:"Prioritize the trapped people, send what is needed to each incident and request more resources according to the plan.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: risk to life decides priority, and the room requests support when resources are short."},{text:"Wait until one incident ends before answering the other.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: both need a response; the room manages priorities and support."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: late at night, a resident called 115 about smoke coming from a shop below his flat. The call taker asked first for the location and a landmark, then whether anyone was inside, and kept him on the line. She dispatched a fire engine and a rescue vehicle and informed the ambulance service. Drivers on the main road moved aside, and a parked car had been left clear of the narrow lane, so the engine reached the building quickly. The fire was put out, and back at the station the crew cleaned and refilled the equipment and reported ready.",quiz:[{question:"Why are vehicles and equipment checked at the start of every shift?",options:[{text:"Because the law requires paint to be checked.",correct:!1},{text:"To decide which crew drives which vehicle.",correct:!1},{text:"To count how many vehicles the directorate owns.",correct:!1},{text:"So that a fault is found before an emergency, not during it.",correct:!0}],explanation:"A pump or breathing set that fails during an incident can cost lives."},{question:"What does the call taker in the operations room ask first?",options:[{text:"The location.",correct:!0},{text:"The caller's age.",correct:!1},{text:"The cause of the fire.",correct:!1},{text:"The owner's name.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Without the location no team can be sent; the other details follow."},{question:"A team must reach people trapped in smoke on an upper floor. Which equipment helps them find people in thick smoke?",options:[{text:"A water tanker parked at the entrance.",correct:!1},{text:"A portable generator for lighting.",correct:!1},{text:"A thermal imaging camera.",correct:!0},{text:"A folding stretcher for the injured.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Thermal imaging cameras show people and hot spots through smoke."},{question:"A breathing set is overdue for maintenance but looks fine. What is the right decision?",options:[{text:"Use it this once because time is short.",correct:!1},{text:"Take it out of service until it is maintained.",correct:!0},{text:"Give it to a volunteer to use.",correct:!1},{text:"Use it only for short tasks.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Equipment overdue for maintenance is taken out of service; failure inside smoke can be fatal."},{question:"Which action by the public helps a fire engine reach a building fastest?",options:[{text:"Keeping narrow lanes and building access clear of parked cars.",correct:!0},{text:"Following the fire engine to show the way.",correct:!1},{text:"Calling 115 several times to make them hurry.",correct:!1},{text:"Standing in the road to wave at the crew.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Clear access lets the engine reach and set up quickly; repeated calls block the line."},{question:"Someone calls 115 as a joke. What is the main harm?",options:[{text:"The phone bill of the caller increases.",correct:!1},{text:"The operations room has to write a report.",correct:!1},{text:"A team may be sent away from a real emergency.",correct:!0},{text:"The caller may be recognized by voice.",correct:!1}],explanation:"False calls take the line and the teams away from people who really need them."}]},{id:14,code:"OPS-CAR-114",icon:"Award",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Being a civil defense member: ethics, safety, fitness and careers",subtitle:"The person behind the uniform, and a summary of the course",summary:"Civil Defense work depends on people who are honest, safe, fit and well trained. In this lesson you learn the values of a Civil Defense member, the culture of safety, fitness and health, the paths of training and work, and you review the whole course on the work of Civil Defense.",blocks:[{heading:"Values and ethics",body:`A Civil Defense member serves everyone. People open their homes to the team at the worst moment of their lives, and they must be able to trust it completely. The core values are:

• Humanity: help is given to protect life and reduce suffering.
• Impartiality: help goes to everyone according to need, without discrimination.
• Integrity: no gifts or bribes, and nothing is ever taken from a scene; property found is handed over according to procedure.
• Respect for dignity and privacy: no photos of victims and no sharing of personal information.
• Discipline: lawful orders are followed and the chain of command is respected.
• Responsibility for the uniform and the sign: they are used only for the service, on duty and off duty, including on social media.`},{heading:"A culture of safety",body:"An injured member cannot rescue anyone and becomes another casualty for the team. Safety is therefore part of every task, not an extra. Members wear their protective equipment fully, work in pairs and never enter a danger area alone, check in and out so that the commander always knows who is where, and stop work when conditions become too dangerous. During long or hot operations, members rotate: they rest, drink water, eat and are checked before returning to work. Near misses and small injuries are reported, because each report helps prevent a serious accident. Safe driving of emergency vehicles is part of the same culture."},{heading:"Fitness and health",body:"Civil defense work is physically hard: members carry heavy equipment, climb stairs in heat and smoke and work for long hours with breathing apparatus. Regular exercise, healthy food, enough sleep and not smoking help members do this work safely, and medical checks follow the rules of the institution. The mind needs care too. Members see injury and death, and stress after a hard incident is a normal human reaction. Talking with colleagues, a supervisor or a professional, and noticing changes in sleep, mood or temper, are signs of strength, not weakness. Families also need support, because shift work and danger affect them as well."},{heading:"Training and careers",body:"Every member starts with basic training and continues training throughout their service, because skills fade and methods change. The work of a directorate offers many paths, matching its departments: firefighting, land rescue, water rescue, hazardous materials, first aid, the emergency operations room, prevention, inspection and safety, media and awareness, education and training, logistics and equipment, relief and shelter, and administration. Experienced members can become instructors and leaders. Applications for employment are made through the official announcements of Civil Defense, and the conditions of age, education, health and tests are set by the Directorate. This lesson does not state those conditions; ask the Directorate for current information."},{heading:"Summary of the course",body:"This section has followed the work of Civil Defense from its purpose to the people who carry it out. Lessons 1 and 2 explained the mission, legal basis and organization of a directorate. Lessons 3 to 7 followed the response: the 115 cycle, firefighting, rescue, medical support, and warning, shelter and evacuation. Lessons 8 and 9 covered hazardous materials and the disaster management cycle. Lessons 10 and 11 showed prevention and the role of volunteers and the community. Lessons 12 and 13 explained coordination with other agencies, vehicles, equipment and the operations room. Five messages run through all of them: life comes first, the team's safety comes first, work is organized under one command, prevention protects more lives than response, and the public is a partner in every phase."}],steps:[{title:"Arrive fit and ready",detail:"Come to your shift rested, check your protective equipment and report any health problem.",critical:!0},{title:"Check in and listen to the briefing",detail:"Know your team, your supervisor and your tasks before you start."},{title:"Work safely in your team",detail:"Wear full protective equipment, work in pairs and keep the commander informed of where you are.",critical:!0},{title:"Treat everyone with respect",detail:"Serve every person impartially, protect their privacy and hand over any property you find."},{title:"Rest and recover",detail:"In long operations, take your rotation: rest, drink, eat and be checked before returning."},{title:"Report, learn and look after yourself",detail:"Report injuries and near misses, take part in the review and talk to someone if an incident stays with you."}],warnings:["Never accept gifts or take anything from a scene: it destroys public trust in Civil Defense and is a serious offense.","Do not remove protective equipment in a danger area to work faster: one breath of smoke or one burn can end your service.","Do not hide exhaustion, injury or distress: a member who cannot work safely endangers the whole team."],tips:["Train a little every day: stairs, carrying and endurance help you in the real work.","Check your protective equipment as if your life depends on it, because it does.","Keep learning: ask for training in a second specialization when you can.","Agree with a colleague to check on each other after difficult incidents."],activity:{prompt:'During firefighting in a shop, a member sees cash on the floor. A colleague says: "Nobody will notice, take some." What is the right action?',options:[{text:"Take a little, since the shop is damaged anyway.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: taking property from a scene is theft and destroys trust in the whole service."},{text:"Leave it on the floor and say nothing.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: the money may be lost or taken by others; it must be handed over according to procedure."},{text:"Give it to the colleague to deal with later.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: handing it to someone outside the procedure does not protect the owner."},{text:"Refuse, secure the money and hand it to the commander according to procedure so it is recorded and returned.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: integrity means nothing is taken and property found is handed over and recorded."}]},caseStudy:[{prompt:"After several hours at a large fire on a hot day, a member feels dizzy and very tired but does not want to look weak. What should he do?",options:[{text:"Keep working until the fire is out.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: exhaustion and heat can make him collapse in a dangerous place."},{text:"Tell his supervisor at once and go to the rest area to rest, drink and be checked.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: rotation and rest protect the member and the team."},{text:"Remove his protective jacket and continue working.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: removing protection in the danger area exposes him to burns and smoke."}]},{prompt:"A week later, the same member cannot sleep and keeps seeing images from the fire. What is the best step?",options:[{text:"Talk with his supervisor, a trusted colleague or a professional, and use any support the institution offers.",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct: stress after a hard incident is normal and support helps recovery."},{text:"Hide it so that no one doubts his ability.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: hiding distress can make it worse and affect his safety at work."},{text:"Resign immediately without telling anyone.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: with support, most people recover; a sudden decision is not needed."},{text:"Watch videos of the fire to get used to it.",correct:!1,feedback:"Not correct: repeating the images does not help and may increase distress."}]}],realCase:"Illustrative scenario: at a long night operation after a building collapse, the commander set up a rest area and rotated the teams. One young member at first refused to rest, but his team leader insisted. He drank, ate and was checked, and returned to work alert. At the end of the shift the team talked together about what they had seen. Later the young member said that the talk helped him sleep. The team had worked many hours without any injury, and the people they rescued thanked them for their respect and care.",quiz:[{question:"What does impartiality mean for a Civil Defense member?",options:[{text:"Help goes to everyone by need alone.",correct:!0},{text:"Help goes first to people the member knows.",correct:!1},{text:"Help goes first to the people who pay.",correct:!1},{text:"Help goes only to citizens of the same city.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Impartiality means need alone decides who is helped first."},{question:"A member finds jewelry while searching a burnt house. What is right?",options:[{text:"Keep it safe at home until the owner asks.",correct:!1},{text:"Give it to a neighbor who knows the family.",correct:!1},{text:"Hand it to the commander to be recorded.",correct:!0},{text:"Leave it where it is without telling anyone.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Property found is handed over and recorded so it can be returned to the owner."},{question:"Why do members rotate and rest during long operations?",options:[{text:"To give the media time to film.",correct:!1},{text:"Because exhaustion and heat cause injuries and mistakes.",correct:!0},{text:"Because the law requires a fixed break every hour.",correct:!1},{text:"To let volunteers take over the hot zone.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Rest, water and food restore members so that they can work safely."},{question:"Which behavior on social media fits the values of a Civil Defense member?",options:[{text:"Posting photos of victims to show the hard work.",correct:!1},{text:"Sharing unconfirmed numbers of casualties.",correct:!1},{text:"Wearing the uniform in a commercial advertisement.",correct:!1},{text:"Sharing only official safety messages from Civil Defense.",correct:!0}],explanation:"The uniform and information are used only for the service and with respect for privacy."},{question:"Where should someone who wants a job with Civil Defense get the conditions?",options:[{text:"From a friend who once worked there.",correct:!1},{text:"From a post on social media.",correct:!1},{text:"From the Directorate's official announcements.",correct:!0},{text:"From any member met at an incident.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Conditions of employment are set by the Directorate and published officially."},{question:"Which message runs through the whole course on the work of Civil Defense?",options:[{text:"Life comes first and the team's safety comes first.",correct:!0},{text:"Property is protected before people.",correct:!1},{text:"Each agency should work alone.",correct:!1},{text:"Response is more important than prevention.",correct:!1}],explanation:"Every lesson rests on life first, team safety, one command, prevention and the public as partner."}]}],t={lessons:e};export{t as default,e as lessons};
